__kbPart({"name":"XREAL","content":{"XREAL-Developer-Guide":"---\ntype: dev-guide\ncompany: \"XREAL\"\nslug: XREAL\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, dev-guide]\n---\n# XREAL — Developer Guide (hackathon edition)\n\n⬆ [XREAL profile](XREAL.md) · [Product portfolio](XREAL-Products.md) · [Roadmap and strategy](XREAL-Roadmap-and-Strategy.md)\n\n> XREAL is an XR vendor, not a robot vendor. The robot-centric headings of the template are adapted: \"control levels\" becomes \"integration levels\", and so on. Research note: live web search was unavailable for this guide; everything below comes from direct reads of the official docs, the official GitHub org, Android / Unity pages and the sources already used in the profile.\n\n## TL;DR — how you program it\n\n- **Three stacks, do not mix them up:** (1) no code — glasses are a **USB-C DisplayPort monitor**; (2) **XREAL SDK 3.1.0** — a **Unity (C#)** package for One series / Air 2 Ultra, app runs on an **Android host** (Beam Pro or Samsung S24 / S25); (3) **Android XR** (Unity 6.5 + OpenXR) — **AURA only**.\n- **Check the glasses model before you plan features.** Per the official compatibility table: **One series + XREAL Eye = 6DoF head pose + RGB camera, but no hand tracking, planes, image tracking, depth mesh or anchors.** Those need **Air 2 Ultra** (XREAL SDK) or **AURA** (Android XR).\n- **Fastest 48-hour route:** render your operator UI (RViz, web dashboard, camera feed) on a laptop or robot computer and show it in the glasses. Zero SDK work, works in 5 minutes.\n- **No official ROS 2 package, no Python / C++ / Unreal SDK, no simulation assets, no robot models.** Robot integration = your Unity app talks to ROS 2 over a bridge (ROS-TCP-Connector, rosbridge WebSocket or plain UDP).\n- **Hard gotchas:** do **not** update the host to **Android 16**; turn off **Samsung DeX**; 6DoF on One + Eye needs the **latest glasses firmware**; the host's USB-C port is occupied by the glasses, so debug over **wireless ADB**.\n\n## SDKs and interfaces\n\n| SDK / repo | Language | What it controls | Link | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **USB-C DP Alt Mode** (no SDK) | none | Glasses as a 1080p / 1200p external display; on X1 glasses (One, One Pro, 1 Pro+, 1S, R1) the screen can be anchored in 3DoF by the glasses themselves | — | ✅ Works with any DP-Alt-Mode host. Check your laptop / SBC port first |\n| **XREAL SDK 3.1.0 for Unity** (`com.xreal.xr.tar.gz`) | C# (Unity 2021.3 / 2022.3 / 6000.0 LTS) | Head tracking (0 / 3 / 6DoF), hand tracking, planes, image tracking, depth mesh, spatial anchors, RGB camera, recording, phone-as-controller — **feature set depends on glasses model** (table below) | [docs](https://docs.xreal.com/) · [download](https://developer.xreal.com/download) | ✅ Released 25 Nov 2025. Built on Unity XR Plug-in framework, AR Foundation, XR Interaction Toolkit, XR Hands. Replaces NRSDK 2.x |\n| **ControlGlasses 3.1.0** (APK) | — | Companion app on Samsung phones: switches glasses to 3D mode and launches SDK apps | [release note](https://docs.xreal.com/Release%20Note/XREAL%20SDK%203.1.0) | ✅ Required on phones. On Beam Pro the equivalent is **MyGlasses 1.11.0** |\n| **First Person View + StreamingReceiver V1.2.0** | Unity prefab + Windows exe | Record or stream the wearer's view to a **Windows PC** on the same LAN | [docs](https://docs.xreal.com/Tools/First%20Person%20View) | ✅ Real-world video only with One series + Eye; other glasses stream virtual content only |\n| **Simulator (Unity Editor)** | C# | Test scenes in the Editor without glasses (uses Unity's XR Device Simulator) | [docs](https://docs.xreal.com/Tools/Emulator) | ✅ |\n| **CloudXR client** | — | Stream **SteamVR** content from a Windows PC with NVIDIA GPU to the glasses over Wi-Fi | [docs](https://docs.xreal.com/Tools/XR%20Streaming) | ✅ For One series use CloudXR server **3.2** (4.0.1 crashes, will not be fixed) |\n| **MRTK3 integration** | C# | Microsoft MRTK3 UI / interaction on top of XREAL SDK | [docs](https://docs.xreal.com/MRTK3_Integration) | ✅ Documented; the GitHub fork was last updated Sep 2024 |\n| **Android XR — Unity** (Unity OpenXR: Android XR + Android XR Extensions for Unity) | C# (Unity 6.5) | **AURA only:** hand tracking, planes, anchors (persistent / shareable), marker tracking, occlusion, dimming control, puck trackpad input | [Android docs](https://developer.android.com/develop/xr/unity) · [Unity blog](https://unity.com/blog/unity-android-xr-wired-glasses-support) · [extensions repo](https://github.com/android/android-xr-unity-package) | ✅ OpenXR + **Vulkan** + URP recommended. **No eye tracking on AURA**. One APK can target Galaxy XR and AURA |\n| **Android XR — other paths** (Jetpack XR SDK, native OpenXR, WebXR) | Kotlin / C++ / JS | AURA only | [Android XR](https://developer.android.com/develop/xr) | Listed by Google for Android XR in general; per-feature support on AURA ⚠️ unverified |\n| `nreal-ai` GitHub org | mixed | 7 public repos, mostly legacy NRSDK samples and WebXR forks; newest is `uvc_android` (\"uvc test with puck\", C++, Jun 2026) | [GitHub](https://github.com/nreal-ai) | The SDK itself is **not** on GitHub — download the tarball |\n| Community Linux drivers (unofficial) | Rust / C | Read head IMU over USB on Linux without Android | [XRLinuxDriver](https://github.com/wheaney/XRLinuxDriver) · [ar-drivers-rs](https://github.com/badicsalex/ar-drivers-rs) | ⚠️ unverified for current One-series / 1 Pro+ firmware; not checked today; no XREAL support |\n\n**Feature matrix — XREAL SDK 3.1.0 (official compatibility page):**\n\n| Feature | One series + XREAL Eye | Air / Air 2 / Air 2 Pro | Air 2 Ultra |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Head tracking | **6DoF** (3DoF without Eye) | 3DoF | **6DoF** |\n| Hand tracking | No | No | **Yes** |\n| Plane detection | No | No | **Yes** |\n| Image tracking | No | No | **Yes** |\n| Depth mesh | No | No | **Yes** |\n| Spatial anchors | No | No | **Yes** |\n| RGB camera frames | **Yes** (YUV_420_888) | No camera | No (dual grayscale SLAM cameras, not exposed as RGB) |\n| First-person view | App + real world | App only | App only |\n| Phone as 3DoF controller | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Editor simulator | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n\n1 Pro+ is not yet listed in the SDK docs (they stop at One / One Pro / 1S) — SDK behaviour on 1 Pro+ ⚠️ unverified; ask at the booth.\n\n## Control levels\n\nThere is no robot to control; think of four **integration levels**, from cheapest to deepest.\n\n| Level | What you get | Effort | Hardware needed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **L0 — Monitor** | Private big screen for any UI. Refresh up to **120 Hz** (R1: 240 Hz) | 5 min | Any glasses + DP-Alt-Mode host |\n| **L1 — On-glasses 3DoF (X1 chip)** | Screen stays fixed in space while the head turns; about **3 ms** motion-to-photon. No app code, but also **no pose output to your software** | 5 min | One / One Pro / 1 Pro+ / 1S / R1 |\n| **L2 — XREAL SDK app (Unity)** | Head pose (3 / 6DoF) in your code, world-anchored 3D content, phone controller, RGB frames (One + Eye), hands / planes / anchors (Air 2 Ultra only) | 0.5–1 day | Glasses + Beam Pro or Samsung S24 / S25 |\n| **L3 — Android XR app** | Full spatial app: 6DoF, hands, planes, anchors, markers, Gemini, Play Store | 0.5–1 day (if you know Unity 6 / OpenXR) | AURA + its puck |\n\n- **Rates:** pose and hand-tracking update rates are **not public** in the docs. Design for display rate (60–120 Hz) and measure on device.\n- **Developer / EDU editions:** none. There is no paid licence tier; the SDK download is free. On Beam Pro you must enable **developer mode** (tap the glasses icon in MyGlasses 10 times with no glasses connected) to pick your own MR app.\n- **\"Low-level\" access:** no official raw-IMU or raw-SLAM-camera API. Lowest official level is Unity `XRInputSubsystem` head pose, XR Hands joints and YUV camera textures.\n\n## ROS 2 and middleware\n\n- **Official ROS 2 support: none.** No package, no message definitions, no URDF.\n- Working patterns for a hackathon:\n\n| Pattern | How | When to use |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Display only** | Run RViz2 / Foxglove / a web UI on the robot PC or laptop, plug the glasses in | Operator HUD, fleet dashboard — default choice |\n| **Unity ↔ ROS 2 bridge** | Unity [ROS-TCP-Connector](https://github.com/Unity-Technologies/ROS-TCP-Connector) in the app + ROS-TCP-Endpoint node on the robot PC | Publish head pose (`geometry_msgs/PoseStamped`) and hand joints; subscribe to images / status |\n| **rosbridge WebSocket** | `rosbridge_server` on the robot, JSON from a small C# WebSocket client | Low-rate status, commands, no custom message generation |\n| **Plain UDP pose stream** | Send head pose (7 floats) at display rate to a tiny Python node that republishes | Lowest latency head-tracked camera gimbal / teleop |\n| **Video to the glasses** | WebRTC or MJPEG in a Unity texture, or just a browser window at L0 | Robot camera first-person view |\n\n- The bridge repos above are Unity-maintained, not XREAL-specific; they were not re-checked today. Android host and robot must be on the same network — bring your own router or hotspot.\n- Community XR-teleop stacks (OpenTeleVision-style, Quest / Vision Pro based) do **not** support XREAL out of the box.\n\n## Simulation and digital twins\n\n- **Robot simulation assets (URDF / MJCF / USD, Isaac Lab, MuJoCo, Gazebo): none** — not applicable.\n- **App-side simulation:** Unity Editor **Simulator** (XR Device Simulator prefab) for XREAL SDK scenes; for AURA, Android Studio's Android XR emulator exists for Android XR in general — AURA-specific profile ⚠️ unverified.\n- **Digital-twin idea that works:** import the partner robot's URDF into Unity (Unity URDF Importer), drive it from `/joint_states` over the bridge, and overlay it — world-anchored on Air 2 Ultra / AURA, head-locked or 6DoF-fixed on One + Eye.\n\n## AI stack\n\n| Item | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Pretrained robot policies / VLA / foundation models | **None** from XREAL |\n| Datasets | **None** published |\n| On-device assistant | **Gemini** built into AURA (Android XR). One series has no on-board AI compute |\n| Teleoperation product | **None** official |\n| LeRobot support | **None** official |\n| Research precedent | \"ActiveGlasses\" (arXiv 2604.08534): smart glasses + stereo camera to record egocentric bare-hand demos, then the camera rides a 6-DoF perception arm. Per the profile, the paper used an Air 2 Ultra for head pose and added its own stereo camera. No code or dataset link on the arXiv page |\n\nDIY data-collection pipeline that is realistic in 48 h:\n\n1. One series + **XREAL Eye**: log **RGB frames (YUV_420_888)** + **6DoF head pose** from Unity, timestamped.\n2. Stream or save (`Application.persistentDataPath`), convert offline to your dataset format (e.g. LeRobot format — your own converter).\n3. Hands are **not** tracked on One + Eye: run an off-board hand-pose model on the RGB stream, or use Air 2 Ultra / AURA for native hand joints (26 joints per the profile's read of the hand-tracking docs).\n\n## Compute, sensors and connectivity\n\n| Device | Compute | Sensors exposed to developers | Connection |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| One / One Pro / 1S (and 1 Pro+ ⚠️) | X1 co-processor only (display + 3DoF). **No app compute** | IMU-based 3DoF pose; with **Eye**: RGB camera + 6DoF | USB-C to host |\n| XREAL Eye | — | RGB camera (resolution / fps not stated in docs) | Clips into One-series glasses |\n| Air 2 Ultra | None on glasses | Dual grayscale SLAM cameras (used internally), IMU; **no RGB** | USB-C to host |\n| Beam Pro | Android handheld; official SDK host | Dual cameras for spatial capture | USB-C to glasses, Wi-Fi |\n| Samsung Galaxy S24 (Android 14) / S25 (Android 15) | Official SDK hosts (only Samsung flagships tested) | — | USB-C to glasses, Wi-Fi |\n| AURA puck | Snapdragon Reality Elite (up to 48 TOPS) + X1S; 4,455 mAh | 6DoF cameras, AI camera, 4 mics; hand tracking | Tethered to glasses; dual USB-C; wired PC mirroring |\n\n- **Laptop → glasses (L0):** one USB-C cable, laptop port must support DisplayPort Alt Mode. Glasses draw power from the host. XREAL Neo (10,000 mAh hub with DP pass-through) helps with battery-limited hosts.\n- **Laptop → host for development:** `adb tcpip 5555` then `adb connect <host-ip>` (wireless ADB), because the host's USB-C port goes to the glasses.\n- **Network defaults:** none (no fixed IPs or ports like a robot). First-person streaming auto-discovers the Windows receiver on the LAN.\n- **Firmware:** update via MyGlasses 1.11.0 (Beam Pro) or the web OTA tool at https://www.xreal.com/ota/ from a PC.\n\n## First 60 minutes checklist\n\n**Path A — display HUD (any glasses, 10 minutes)**\n\n1. Plug glasses into the laptop or robot PC USB-C port; confirm an external display appears.\n2. On X1 glasses, use the on-glasses menu to anchor the screen in space; set brightness / dimming for the room.\n3. Put your UI full-screen: RViz2, Foxglove, a web dashboard or the robot camera feed. Use large fonts and high contrast (optical see-through washes out dark colours — black is transparent).\n4. Walk the demo path once with the cable to check comfort and snag points. Done.\n\n**Path B — XREAL SDK HelloMR + pose to ROS 2 (One + Eye or Air 2 Ultra)**\n\n1. Ask the booth: which glasses, which host, firmware current, Android version (must be **below 16**), is a sample project provided.\n2. Install Unity **2022.3 LTS or 6000.0 LTS** with Android Build Support; Android SDK **API 31+**.\n3. New 3D project → switch platform to Android → install **XR Interaction Toolkit** (2.5.x or 3.0.x) + Starter Assets; AR Foundation and XR Hands only if you use Air 2 Ultra features.\n4. Package Manager → *Add package from tarball* → `com.xreal.xr.tar.gz`. Enable **XREAL** under XR Plug-in Management (Android tab) → Project Validation → **Fix All** (OpenGL ES3, IL2CPP, ARM64, min API Android 10).\n5. XREAL settings: Initial Tracking Type = **6DoF** (or 3DoF), Input Source = Controller (Hands only on Air 2 Ultra), Support Devices = REALITY (6DoF glasses) and / or VISION (3DoF glasses).\n6. Open the **HelloMR** scene (Interaction Basics sample) → Build → `adb install` → unplug from PC → plug glasses into host → launch from **ControlGlasses** (phone) or **MyGlasses** (Beam Pro) → grant \"display over other apps\".\n7. Verify: content stays fixed when you turn (3DoF) and when you step sideways (6DoF). Recenter via `XREALUtility.GetInputSubsystem()?.TryRecenter()`.\n8. Read sensors: log the main camera transform each frame (head pose). On One + Eye open the **RGBCameraAndCapture** sample to see camera frames.\n9. Add a UDP or ROS-TCP sender for the head pose; echo it on the robot PC (`ros2 topic echo`). That is your hello-world integration.\n\n**Path C — AURA (Android XR)**\n\n1. Unity **6.5** → Android XR Build Profile → packages: OpenXR Plugin 1.15+, Unity OpenXR: Android XR, XR Interaction Toolkit 3.5, XR Hands 1.6+, AR Foundation 6.2+.\n2. Start from a Unity XR / Mixed Reality template; graphics API **Vulkan**, URP.\n3. Request `android.permission.HAND_TRACKING` for XR Hands; design for hands + puck trackpad, no eye tracking.\n4. Build and deploy to the puck over ADB; verify hand joints and a plane, then add your network bridge as in Path B.\n\n## Known gotchas and safety\n\n| Gotcha | What to do |\n|---|---|\n| **Android 16 breaks the XREAL SDK** (official warning, still on every docs page) | Do not accept OS updates on dev phones during the event |\n| One + Eye has **6DoF only** — no hands, planes, anchors, image tracking | Use phone controller or head-gaze for input; use markers on the robot side instead |\n| Air 2 Ultra has **no RGB camera** access | Mount a separate camera if you need egocentric video |\n| 6DoF on One + Eye requires latest firmware + SDK 3.1.0 + matching ControlGlasses / MyGlasses | Update before you start coding |\n| Only **Beam Pro and Samsung S24 / S25-class** hosts are supported | Do not plan on your own Pixel / Xiaomi |\n| Samsung **DeX** hijacks the display | Disable DeX on the phone |\n| XREAL SDK wants **OpenGL ES3**; Android XR wants **Vulkan** | One Unity project per stack; do not try to share a build |\n| Overlay content or URP without single-pass | Disable Multithreaded Rendering (per docs) |\n| \"Not supported on this device\" in MyGlasses | Add the `com.nreal.supportDevices` meta-data to `AndroidManifest.xml` (see FAQ) |\n| First-person streaming | Windows receiver only; same LAN; **receiver firewall off**; fails on crowded Wi-Fi → use a hotspot |\n| Camera / UVC permission on newer Android | Request CAMERA at runtime **before** USB permission |\n| Glasses power comes from the host | Bring a power bank or Neo; plan charging between demo runs |\n| Docs lag hardware (1 Pro+, AURA not in docs.xreal.com) | Confirm with XREAL staff on site |\n\nSafety (XREAL has no e-stop, modes or torque limits — the risks are human-factors):\n\n- **Never make the glasses the only safety channel.** Teleoperating another vendor's robot through a Wi-Fi pose stream needs a second person holding the robot's own e-stop, plus a dead-man switch and a pose-timeout (stop if no packet for about 200 ms).\n- Electrochromic dimming and bright virtual screens **reduce situational awareness** — keep lenses clear when walking near robots; operators should stand or sit still.\n- The tether is a **trip and snag hazard**, especially for elderly users (T04). Route the cable behind the ear and down the back; keep sessions short and seated.\n- Camera recording of patients or bystanders (Eye, AURA) needs consent — relevant for clinic scenarios in Leukerbad.\n\n## Community resources\n\n- Official docs: https://docs.xreal.com/ (SDK 3.1.0; switch to 2.4.1 for legacy NRSDK)\n- Downloads and API reference: https://developer.xreal.com/download · https://developer.xreal.com/reference/nrsdk/overview\n- Sample list (HelloMR, PlaneDetection, Anchors, RGBCameraAndCapture, FirstPersonViewStreamingCast, MarkerTracking): https://docs.xreal.com/Sample%20Code\n- GitHub (legacy samples): https://github.com/nreal-ai\n- AURA: Android XR docs https://developer.android.com/develop/xr and the Unity Discussions thread \"XREAL AURA support now available in Unity 6.5\" (Android XR tag)\n- Official developer forum / Discord: exists per XREAL's site, exact current URL not verified today — ask the booth\n- Best resource this weekend: the XREAL staff on site\n\n## Sources\n\n- [XREAL SDK docs — overview (3.1.0)](https://docs.xreal.com/)\n- [XREAL docs — Getting Started with XREAL SDK](https://docs.xreal.com/Getting%20Started%20with%20XREAL%20SDK)\n- [XREAL docs — SDK 3.1.0 release note](https://docs.xreal.com/Release%20Note/XREAL%20SDK%203.1.0)\n- [XREAL docs — Compatibility](https://docs.xreal.com/XREALDevices/Compatibility)\n- [XREAL docs — XREAL Glasses](https://docs.xreal.com/XREALDevices/XREAL%20Glasses)\n- [XREAL docs — Access RGB Camera](https://docs.xreal.com/Camera/Access%20RGB%20Camera)\n- [XREAL docs — Camera](https://docs.xreal.com/Camera/Camera)\n- [XREAL docs — First Person View](https://docs.xreal.com/Tools/First%20Person%20View)\n- [XREAL docs — Tools](https://docs.xreal.com/category/tools)\n- [XREAL docs — CloudXR / XR Streaming](https://docs.xreal.com/Tools/XR%20Streaming)\n- [XREAL docs — Sample Code](https://docs.xreal.com/Sample%20Code)\n- [XREAL docs — Hand Tracking](https://docs.xreal.com/Input%20and%20Interactions/Hand%20Tracking)\n- [XREAL docs — Frequently Asked Questions](https://docs.xreal.com/Frequently%20Asked%20Questions)\n- [GitHub — nreal-ai organisation](https://github.com/nreal-ai)\n- [Android Developers — Develop with Unity for Android XR](https://developer.android.com/develop/xr/unity)\n- [Unity blog — Unity support for XREAL AURA (16 Jun 2026)](https://unity.com/blog/unity-android-xr-wired-glasses-support)\n- [Road to VR — XREAL AURA launch and specs (28 Oct 2026)](https://www.roadtovr.com/google-ar-glasses-xreal-aura-release-date-price/)\n- [arXiv 2604.08534 — ActiveGlasses](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.08534)\n- [XREAL profile in this vault](XREAL.md)\n","XREAL-Products":"---\ntype: products\ncompany: \"XREAL\"\nslug: XREAL\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, products]\n---\n# XREAL — Product Portfolio\n\n⬆ [XREAL profile](XREAL.md) · [Developer guide](XREAL-Developer-Guide.md) · [Roadmap and strategy](XREAL-Roadmap-and-Strategy.md)\n\n> **Read this first:** XREAL sells **wearable displays, not robots**. Every product below is a human-interface add-on for someone else's robot: operator HUD, hands-free instruction overlay, or head / hand pose input. Pick by one question — **do you need only a screen (any model), 6DoF + hands (One series + Eye, or Air 2 Ultra), or a standalone Android XR computer (AURA)?**\n>\n> Verification note: specs were read from the official XREAL and ASUS ROG product pages on 2026-11-14. Web search was unavailable during this pass, so prices and dates not shown on those pages are carried over from the [profile](XREAL.md) and anything else is marked ⚠️ unverified.\n\n## Lineup map\n\nTrack relevance: ● strong · ◐ partial · ○ none\n\n| Product | Category | Status | Launched | Price (if public) | Positioning (one line) | T01 | T02 | T03 | T04 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| XREAL AURA | Android XR see-through glasses + compute puck | ✅ (US / CA / JP / KR; **not yet sold in Europe**) | 28 Oct 2026 | **USD 1,279** (12/256) · 1,499 (16/512); launch offer 1,099 / 1,299 to end of 2026 | Only standalone device: 6DoF, hand tracking, camera, Gemini, apps on the puck | ◐ | ● | ◐ | ● |\n| XREAL 1 Pro+ | Tethered display glasses (flagship) | ✅ | 15 Oct 2026 | **USD 599** | Brightest One-series display (1,200 nits); 6DoF + gestures with Eye | ◐ | ● | ◐ | ● |\n| XREAL One Pro | Tethered display glasses | ✅ (superseded by 1 Pro+) | 2025 | USD 599 | Same 57° optics, 700 nits; the model most SDK material refers to | ◐ | ● | ◐ | ● |\n| XREAL 1S | Tethered display glasses (entry) | ✅ | Jan 2026 | **USD 449** | Lightest spatial model (82 g), 1200p, native 3DoF | ◐ | ◐ | ◐ | ● |\n| ROG XREAL R1 | Gaming display glasses (with ASUS) | ✅ | 2026 | about **USD 849** | 240 Hz gaming display with HDMI / DP dock | ◐ | ◐ | ○ | ○ |\n| XREAL Eye | Clip-in camera module | ✅ | 2025 | **USD 99** | Turns One-series glasses into 6DoF devices; 12 MP POV camera | ◐ | ● | ◐ | ● |\n| XREAL Beam Pro | Android handheld host / spatial camera | ✅ | 2024 | from **USD 199** | Pocket Android computer that runs XREAL SDK apps for the glasses | ◐ | ● | ◐ | ● |\n| XREAL Neo | Battery + video hub | ✅ | Feb 2026 | USD 119 | Powers host and glasses for long sessions | ○ | ◐ | ○ | ◐ |\n| xbx a01+ | Budget display glasses (sub-brand) | ✅ | 2026 | USD 299 ⚠️ unverified | 62 g, very bright, **0DoF only** — a plain head-fixed screen | ○ | ◐ | ○ | ◐ |\n| XREAL Air 2 Ultra | Developer 6DoF glasses (legacy) | ✅ shipped 2024; no longer on the official site menu | Mar 2024 | USD 699 at launch | Native 6DoF + hand tracking without add-ons | ◐ | ● | ◐ | ● |\n\n**Fast pick**\n\n| You want | Take | Effort |\n|---|---|---|\n| Operator screen for a robot UI you already have | Any glasses, plugged into a USB-C DisplayPort host | Minutes |\n| Screen that stays fixed in space while the head turns | 1 Pro+, One Pro, 1S, R1 (3DoF on the glasses) | Minutes |\n| Head pose / hand pose as robot input, world-anchored overlays | One series + Eye + Beam Pro, or Air 2 Ultra + Beam Pro (Unity, XREAL SDK) | Half a day or more |\n| Standalone app with Gemini, camera and hand tracking | AURA (Android XR) | Half a day or more; availability on site uncertain |\n\n## Products\n\n### XREAL AURA\n\n![XREAL AURA](assets/xreal-aura.jpg)\n\nOptical see-through glasses wired to a pocket compute puck running **Android XR**. The only XREAL product that runs apps itself and the only one with built-in 6DoF, hand tracking and an RGB camera. Formerly \"Project Aura\".\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Optics | X Prism, **70° FOV**, optical see-through (no camera passthrough) |\n| Display | Sony Micro-OLED, **1920 × 1200 per eye**, up to **120 Hz**, peak **1,050 nits**, 32 PPD, 100% sRGB |\n| Dimming | Electrochromic, 5 levels, auto / manual |\n| Tracking and sensing | 2 world-facing tracking cameras, **6DoF** spatial anchoring, **hand tracking**, high-resolution RGB camera with privacy LED |\n| Glasses chip | XREAL X1S spatial coprocessor, 3 ms motion-to-photon |\n| Audio | Open-ear speakers (Bose tuning), 4-microphone array |\n| Glasses weight | **95 g** |\n| IPD sizes | M 60–66 mm · L 66–72 mm |\n| Puck compute | Snapdragon Reality Elite, **48 TOPS** AI processing |\n| OS | Android XR (Google Play apps, Gemini assistant) |\n| Memory / storage | 12 GB / 256 GB or 16 GB / 512 GB (UFS 4.0) |\n| Puck battery | **4,455 mAh / 34.84 Wh**; runtime not stated on the product page |\n| Puck ports | USB-C 1: glasses (USB 3.1, **full ADB support**) · USB-C 2: charging (up to 57 W, PD 3.0 / QC 3.0) and **DisplayPort in** |\n| Input | Hands, voice, touchpad on puck; Bluetooth keyboard, mouse, game controller; fingerprint button |\n| Wireless | Wi-Fi 6 / 6E, Bluetooth 5.3; GPS, GLONASS, Galileo, QZSS, BeiDou |\n| Cooling / puck weight | Active fan · **265 g** |\n| IP rating | not public |\n\n- **Variants:** 12 GB / 256 GB and 16 GB / 512 GB; two IPD sizes. No separate developer edition found; development uses the public Android XR SDK (Unity, OpenXR — see the [developer guide](XREAL-Developer-Guide.md)).\n- **Why it matters here:** the puck is a real robot-side companion computer — ADB, Wi-Fi 6E, GNSS, 48 TOPS — and the DP-in port means it can also act as a plain display for a laptop.\n- **Typical applications:** standalone operator app with hand gestures; \"look and ask\" assistant using Gemini; remote-expert view; patient or caregiver guidance overlay.\n- **Caveats:** not yet sold in Europe, so on-site units are uncertain; app access to raw camera frames ⚠️ unverified.\n- Official page: [xreal.com/aura](https://www.xreal.com/aura)\n\n### XREAL 1 Pro+\n\n![XREAL 1 Pro+](assets/xreal-1-pro-plus.jpg)\n\nCurrent flagship tethered display glasses. No app compute on board — it shows whatever a USB-C DisplayPort host sends, with head-stabilised screens handled by the X1 chip in the glasses.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Optics | X Prism, **57° FOV** |\n| Display | Sony Micro-OLED, **1,200 nits** peak, **120 Hz**, AI HDR; Standard / Movie / Reading colour modes |\n| Tracking | **Native 3DoF** (Follow and Anchor modes), 3 ms motion-to-photon; **6DoF anchoring + gesture interaction with XREAL Eye** (sold separately; XREAL notes feature availability may vary) |\n| On-glasses processing | X1 spatial computing chip; 2D-to-3D conversion at 60 fps on the glasses |\n| Dimming | Electrochromic, automatic |\n| Audio | Sound by Bose |\n| Fit | Two IPD sizes, adjustable temples, redesigned nose pad |\n| Weight | not published on the product page |\n| Resolution | not stated on the product page |\n| Connectivity | USB-C (DisplayPort); works with phones, handhelds, consoles, PCs |\n| Battery | None — powered by the host |\n\n- **Variants:** two IPD sizes.\n- **Typical applications:** bright operator HUD usable near windows or outdoors; world-anchored robot dashboard with Eye; teleoperation video view.\n- Official page: [xreal.com/1-pro-plus](https://www.xreal.com/1-pro-plus)\n\n### XREAL One Pro\n\n![XREAL One Pro](assets/xreal-one-pro.jpg)\n\nPrevious flagship, still listed. Optically the same class as the 1 Pro+ but dimmer. Most public SDK and community material refers to this model.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Optics | X Prism, **57° FOV**, virtual screen up to 171 in (at 4 m) |\n| Display | 0.55 in Sony Micro-OLED, **120 Hz**, **700 nits** peak, per-unit colour calibration |\n| Tracking | **Native 3DoF** via X1 chip, 3 ms motion-to-photon; 6DoF anchoring with XREAL Eye |\n| Dimming | Electrochromic, 3 levels (Clear / Shade / Theater) |\n| Audio | Sound by Bose |\n| IPD sizes | 57–66 mm and 66–75 mm |\n| Weight | **87 g** |\n| Connectivity | USB-C; host needs **USB-C with DisplayPort output** (Nintendo Switch needs an extra hub) |\n| Battery | None — powered by the host |\n\n- **Variants:** two IPD sizes.\n- **Typical applications:** same as 1 Pro+ for indoor use.\n- Official page: [xreal.com/one-pro](https://www.xreal.com/one-pro) · [US store](https://us.shop.xreal.com/products/xreal-one-pro)\n\n### XREAL 1S\n\n![XREAL 1S](assets/xreal-1s.jpg)\n\nEntry model of the spatial (X1-chip) line and the lightest of them — the comfortable choice for long wear, for example by a patient or therapist.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Display | Sony Micro-OLED, **1200p** (1920 × 1200), **120 Hz**, **700 nits** peak, 108% sRGB |\n| FOV | not stated on the product page ⚠️ unverified |\n| Tracking | **Native 3DoF** via X1 chip (Anchor, Follow, Ultra-wide modes), 3 ms motion-to-photon |\n| On-glasses processing | 2D-to-3D conversion on the glasses |\n| Dimming | Electrochromic, manual or automatic |\n| Audio | Sound by Bose, multi-microphone array with noise cancellation |\n| Weight | **82 g** |\n| Connectivity | USB-C (DisplayPort) |\n| Add-ons named on the page | XREAL Eye, XREAL Neo, XREAL Beam Pro |\n\n- **Typical applications:** low-cost HUD handed out to several team members; patient-side exercise cues; status overlay for a caregiver.\n- Official page: [xreal.com/1s](https://www.xreal.com/1s)\n\n### ROG XREAL R1\n\n![ROG XREAL R1](assets/xreal-rog-r1.jpg)\n\nGaming glasses co-developed with ASUS ROG. Relevant mainly because the included dock accepts **HDMI and DisplayPort**, so hosts without USB-C DP Alt Mode can drive it.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Display | 0.55 in Sony Micro-OLED, **1920 × 1080 per eye**, 120 Hz native, **240 Hz** with Frame Rate Boost (achieved by image scaling) |\n| FOV | **57°**, virtual screen up to 171 in at 4 m, 40 PPD |\n| Brightness | 700 nits max, 106% sRGB |\n| Tracking | Native 3DoF, 3 ms motion-to-photon |\n| Dimming | Electrochromic, 3 levels |\n| Audio | Bose-tuned speakers, noise-cancelling microphone array |\n| Weight | **91 g** (ASUS lists 0.09 kg net) |\n| IPD sizes | 57–66 mm and 66–75 mm |\n| Glasses port | 1 × USB-C (DP Alt Mode) |\n| ROG Control Dock (included) | **2 × HDMI 2.0, 1 × DisplayPort 1.4**, USB-C front (DP Alt Mode), USB-C data and power; 0.23 kg |\n\n- **Typical applications:** low-latency first-person video view for teleoperation from a PC with HDMI / DP only.\n- Official pages: [ASUS ROG spec](https://rog.asus.com/glasses/rog-xreal-r1/spec/) · [xreal.com/rog-xreal-r1](https://www.xreal.com/rog-xreal-r1)\n\n### XREAL Eye\n\n![XREAL Eye](assets/xreal-eye.jpg)\n\nTiny camera module that plugs into the bridge of One-series glasses. This is the part that makes the cheap glasses interesting for robotics: it upgrades them from 3DoF to **6DoF**.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Function | **6DoF spatial anchoring** for One-series glasses, processed by the X1 chip — works with any DisplayPort host, no app needed |\n| Camera | **12 MP**, first-person photo and video |\n| Weight | **1.35 g** |\n| Latency | 3 ms motion-to-photon (X1 chip) |\n| Compatibility | XREAL One series only |\n| Recording | First-person capture needs Beam Pro (per XREAL) |\n\n- **Developer notes:** 6DoF pose is exposed through XREAL SDK 3.1.0+ on an Android host (see [developer guide](XREAL-Developer-Guide.md)). XREAL advertises gesture interaction for 1 Pro+ with Eye; SDK hand-tracking support on One + Eye and raw camera-frame access ⚠️ unverified — ask at the booth.\n- **Typical applications:** head-pose driven camera / gimbal control on a robot; world-locked dashboards; POV recording for demonstrations.\n- Official page: [xreal.com/eye](https://www.xreal.com/eye)\n\n### XREAL Beam Pro\n\n![XREAL Beam Pro](assets/xreal-beam-pro.jpg)\n\nPhone-shaped Android device built as the host for XREAL glasses. For a hackathon it is the **default deployment target for XREAL SDK (Unity) apps** and a self-contained, pocketable operator computer.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| OS | Android with nebulaOS spatial UI; full Google Play Store |\n| Compute | Qualcomm Snapdragon \"spatial companion\" processor (exact model not stated on the page) |\n| Screen | 6.5 in Full HD LCD touch screen |\n| Cameras | **Dual 50 MP**, 50 mm lens spacing, for 3D spatial photo / video |\n| Output to glasses | 1080p, up to 90 Hz |\n| Ports | **Dual USB-C** (glasses and charging at the same time), microSD up to 1 TB |\n| Wireless | Wi-Fi 6, Bluetooth 5.2; optional **5G** variant (SIM required) |\n| Charging | 27 W |\n| Tracking modes | 3DoF with Air / Air 2 / Air 2 Pro / One / One Pro; **full 6DoF with Air 2 Ultra** |\n| Battery capacity | not stated on the product page |\n\n- **Variants and prices:** 128 GB Wi-Fi **USD 199** · 256 GB Wi-Fi USD 249 · 256 GB 5G USD 299; bundles with One or One Pro.\n- **Typical applications:** runs your Unity app and streams poses to a robot over Wi-Fi; 5G variant for outdoor demos away from the venue network; stereo capture of a scene.\n- Official page: [xreal.com/beampro](https://www.xreal.com/beampro)\n\n### XREAL Neo\n\n![XREAL Neo](assets/xreal-neo.jpg)\n\nBattery pack and video hub that sits between a host device and the glasses, so both stay powered. Useful if a demo must run for hours from a phone or handheld.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Function | Power bank + display hub for XREAL glasses (XREAL calls it an \"AR charging expansion dock\") |\n| Capacity, ports, weight | not confirmed from official pages ⚠️ unverified |\n| Price | USD 119 (USD 99 at launch), per the [profile](XREAL.md) |\n\n- Official page: [xreal.com/neo](https://www.xreal.com/neo) (thin on technical detail)\n\n### xbx a01+\n\n![xbx a01+](assets/xreal-xbx-a01-plus.jpg)\n\nBudget sub-brand glasses. Very light and very bright, but **0DoF**: the image is fixed in front of the eyes with no anchoring and no SDK-relevant tracking. XREAL itself points to 1S / One Pro for spatial features.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Weight | **62 g** (56 g without the detachable front frame) |\n| Display | Up to **1,600 nits** peak, native HDR10, real-time HDR upscaling |\n| Virtual screen | 147 in (as advertised) |\n| Tracking | **0DoF** (screen stays in front of the eyes) |\n| Frame | Ultralight nylon, flexible temples, hinges flex up to 20° |\n| Connectivity | Plug-in to phone, tablet, handheld or laptop |\n| FOV, resolution, refresh rate | not confirmed ⚠️ unverified |\n| Price | USD 299 ⚠️ unverified (matches the reported sub-brand entry price) |\n\n- **Typical applications:** cheapest, lightest heads-up status display, including outdoors in bright snow light (washes out in direct sun, per XREAL).\n- Official page: [xreal.com/xbxa01](https://www.xreal.com/xbxa01)\n\n### XREAL Air 2 Ultra\n\nOlder developer-oriented glasses with **native 6DoF and hand tracking** from two built-in tracking cameras — no Eye module needed. Still the reference \"6DoF glasses\" in the XREAL SDK and used in published robotics research (see [profile](XREAL.md)). No longer in the official site menu; no official image could be retrieved.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Tracking | Native **6DoF**, hand tracking, plane detection (via XREAL SDK on an Android host) |\n| Host | Beam Pro gives \"full-featured 6DoF\" per the Beam Pro page |\n| Display, FOV, weight | not re-checked against an official page today ⚠️ unverified |\n| Price | USD 699 at launch (Mar 2024) |\n\n- **Caveat:** a 2026 research paper reported that raw camera frames were not exposed to apps on this model.\n- Official page: none currently live on [xreal.com](https://www.xreal.com)\n\n## Accessories and ecosystem\n\n| Item | What it adds | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| XREAL Eye | 6DoF + 12 MP POV camera for One series | ✅ USD 99 |\n| XREAL Beam Pro | Android host for SDK apps, dual 50 MP spatial camera, optional 5G | ✅ from USD 199 |\n| XREAL Neo | Battery + video hub | ✅ |\n| ROG Control Dock | HDMI 2.0 × 2 and DisplayPort 1.4 input for the R1 (included with R1) | ✅ |\n| XREAL Hub | Adapter needed for hosts such as Nintendo Switch (named in One Pro footnotes) | ✅ details not checked |\n| Prescription lens inserts | Via partner opticians (US / EU / UK) | ✅ |\n| X1 / X1S chips | On-glasses spatial coprocessors (3DoF / 6DoF, 3 ms latency) — not sold separately | ✅ inside products |\n| XREAL SDK 3.x (Unity) | 6DoF, planes, images, hands, anchors for Eye / Air 2 Ultra on Android hosts | ✅ free download |\n| Android XR SDK | Development path for AURA (Unity, OpenXR) | ✅ |\n\nHost checklist for the plain-display route: the host port must output **DisplayPort over USB-C**. Many robot computers and dev boards expose only HDMI — then you need an HDMI-to-USB-C DP adapter with power, the R1 dock, or a laptop in between.\n\n## Legacy and discontinued\n\n- **XREAL One** (2024–25, first X1-chip model) — still referenced as a compatible device on Beam Pro and Eye pages; no longer in the main menu.\n- **XREAL Air / Air 2 / Air 2 Pro** — earlier display glasses without the X1 chip; 3DoF only through a host such as Beam Pro.\n- **XREAL Air 2 Ultra** — see above; legacy but still the most direct 6DoF + hands developer device.\n- **XREAL Beam** (original screen-anchoring box) and **Nreal Light** — predecessors; not relevant here.\n\n## Image credits\n\n| File | Shows | Source URL |\n|---|---|---|\n| assets/xreal-aura.jpg | AURA glasses with compute puck | https://cms-media.xreal.com/catalog_aura_3457d806be.png |\n| assets/xreal-1-pro-plus.jpg | XREAL 1 Pro+ | https://cms-media.xreal.com/catalog_xreal_1_pro_plus_da49cc29ea.png |\n| assets/xreal-one-pro.jpg | XREAL One Pro | https://resource.xreal.com/www-xreal-com/images/home/menu_OnePro.png |\n| assets/xreal-1s.jpg | XREAL 1S | https://cms-media.xreal.com/catalog_xreal_1s_2436ad7c05.png |\n| assets/xreal-rog-r1.jpg | ROG XREAL R1 | https://dlcdnwebimgs.asus.com/gain/D55C57D4-9E20-4023-92A3-C00C874C10B2 |\n| assets/xreal-eye.jpg | XREAL Eye camera module | https://resource.xreal.com/www-xreal-com/images/home/menu_Eye.png |\n| assets/xreal-beam-pro.jpg | XREAL Beam Pro | https://resource.xreal.com/www-xreal-com/images/home/menu_BeamPro.png |\n| assets/xreal-neo.jpg | XREAL Neo | https://resource.xreal.com/www-xreal-com/images/home/menu_XREAL_Neo.png |\n| assets/xreal-xbx-a01-plus.jpg | xbx a01+ | https://cms-media.xreal.com/catalog_xbx_a01_plus_a7fa719864.png |\n\nAll images © XREAL (R1 image © ASUS), taken from the official sites / CDNs and converted to JPEG. No official image was found for XREAL Air 2 Ultra.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [XREAL — home page and product menu](https://www.xreal.com)\n- [XREAL AURA — product page and spec sheet](https://www.xreal.com/aura)\n- [XREAL 1 Pro+ — product page](https://www.xreal.com/1-pro-plus)\n- [XREAL One Pro — product page](https://www.xreal.com/one-pro)\n- [XREAL 1S — product page](https://www.xreal.com/1s)\n- [ROG XREAL R1 — XREAL product page](https://www.xreal.com/rog-xreal-r1)\n- [ASUS ROG — ROG XREAL R1 specifications](https://rog.asus.com/glasses/rog-xreal-r1/spec/)\n- [XREAL Eye — product page](https://www.xreal.com/eye)\n- [XREAL Beam Pro — product page](https://www.xreal.com/beampro)\n- [XREAL Neo — product page](https://www.xreal.com/neo)\n- [xbx a01+ — product page](https://www.xreal.com/xbxa01)\n- [XREAL profile in this repository (prices, launch dates, SDK notes and their sources)](XREAL.md)\n","XREAL-Roadmap-and-Strategy":"---\ntype: roadmap\ncompany: \"XREAL\"\nslug: XREAL\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, roadmap]\n---\n# XREAL — Roadmap and Strategy\n\n⬆ [XREAL profile](XREAL.md) · [Product portfolio](XREAL-Products.md) · [Developer guide](XREAL-Developer-Guide.md)\n\n> Research note: live web search was unavailable for this file. It is built from direct reads of the sources listed at the end (trade press, official docs, GitHub) plus the profile. **Nothing after the 28 Oct 2026 AURA launch has been checked** — in particular the Hong Kong listing outcome and AURA's European date.\n\n## Company trajectory\n\nRows marked † are background knowledge, not re-checked against a source today.\n\n| Date | Milestone |\n|---|---|\n| 2017 | Founded as **Nreal** in Beijing by Dr. Chi Xu (Xu Chi) |\n| 2019–2022 † | Nreal Light (first consumer AR glasses with cameras), then the Air line of lightweight display glasses |\n| 2023 † | Renamed **XREAL**; Air 2 / Air 2 Pro |\n| Mar 2024 | **Air 2 Ultra** ships — 6DoF developer-oriented glasses (USD 699) |\n| 2024 † | **Beam Pro** Android host; first **One series** glasses with the in-house **X1** spatial chip (late 2024) |\n| May 2025 | **USD 28.6M** round led by Pudong Venture Capital |\n| May 2025 † | **Project Aura** with Google (Android XR) first announced |\n| 25 Nov 2025 | **XREAL SDK 3.1.0** — 6DoF on One series via the XREAL Eye camera |\n| Jan 2026 (CES) | **XREAL 1S** (USD 449), **Neo** power / video hub, **ROG XREAL R1** with ASUS (240 Hz); multi-year extension of the Google partnership — XREAL named **lead hardware partner for Android XR** on optical see-through devices |\n| Jan 2026 | **USD 100M** round at a reported **USD 1B** valuation (supply-chain partners among investors); about **USD 433M** raised in total |\n| 1 Apr 2026 | **Hong Kong main-board IPO application** (sponsors CICC, Citi). 2025 revenue about **RMB 516M** (+30.8% YoY), gross margin up from under 20% to **above 35%** (2023→2025), **>70% of revenue overseas** |\n| 19 May 2026 | Google I/O: Android XR developer early-access programme for Aura hardware |\n| 16 Jun 2026 | Unity 6.5 adds AURA support (first XR glasses supported by Unity on Android XR) |\n| 15 Oct 2026 | **XREAL 1 Pro+** on sale (USD 599) |\n| 28 Oct 2026 | **XREAL AURA** launches (from USD 1,279; promo USD 1,099); first batch ships **mid-Nov 2026** in US, Canada, Japan, South Korea |\n\n## Current focus projects\n\n| Focus | What is happening now | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| **AURA rollout** | First shipments this month; 150+ made-for-XR apps; Best Buy + own US shop; accessories (GameSir controller, keyboard) | Road to VR, 28 Oct 2026 |\n| **Android XR ecosystem** | Lead hardware partner; developer programme with Google; Unity support live | GamesBeat, Unity blog |\n| **One-series refresh** | 1S (entry), 1 Pro+ (flagship, 1,200 nits), R1 (gaming with ASUS) — all on the in-house X1 chip | Profile sources |\n| **In-house silicon** | X1 on glasses; **X1S** co-processor inside the AURA puck next to Snapdragon Reality Elite | Road to VR |\n| **Capital markets** | HKEX listing targeted **by end of 2026** (CEO statement, Jun 2026). Outcome ⚠️ unverified | Caixin, UC Today |\n| **Budget sub-brand (XBX)** | Listed in site navigation; details ⚠️ unverified | Profile |\n| **Developer SDK for One series** | SDK 3.1.0 is a year old; docs do not yet cover 1 Pro+ or AURA; Android 16 incompatibility still flagged | docs.xreal.com |\n\nNot a focus (no public evidence): robotics, teleoperation, healthcare, industrial or public-sector deployments.\n\n## Announced and upcoming products\n\n| Item | Status | Timing |\n|---|---|---|\n| AURA — first-wave deliveries (US, CA, JP, KR) | ✅ / shipping starts | mid-Nov 2026 |\n| AURA — **Europe** | 🧪 announced as a later wave | \"soon after\" the first wave per the profile's sources; no date. Expect few or no retail units in Switzerland today |\n| AURA — wider retail incl. select **Google Store** locations (US, Japan) | 🧪 | 2027 |\n| Hong Kong listing | 🧪 filed 1 Apr 2026 | target end-2026; ⚠️ unverified whether completed |\n| ROG XREAL R1 | ✅ pre-orders opened; announced to ship H1 2026 | actual ship date ⚠️ unverified |\n| Subscription / AI-assistant revenue | 🔭 concept mentioned by the CEO | no date |\n| Next One-series generation, next X-chip | not public | — |\n| SDK update covering 1 Pro+ / Android 16 | not public | — |\n| Robotics or teleoperation SDK | not public (nothing announced) | — |\n\n## Strategic direction and markets\n\n- **Positioning:** consumer \"wearable display\" maker for entertainment, gaming and productivity — explicitly **not** enterprise headsets. Lightweight glasses tethered to phones, PCs, consoles.\n- **Two product lines:** (1) affordable display glasses with own chips (One series, XBX); (2) premium spatial computing on **Android XR + Gemini** (AURA).\n- **AI thesis (CEO, Jun 2026):** glasses are the natural always-on carrier for AI because they see what the user sees; he forecasts **20% penetration by 2030** from under 1% today. Revenue is hardware-only now.\n- **Platform dependence by design:** the CEO's view is that only Google and Apple can build a global device ecosystem — XREAL rides Google's.\n- **Markets:** sales in about **40 countries and regions**; North America (US largest), Europe and parts of Asia named as key markets. Claims **No. 1 in AR-glasses revenue since 2022** and No. 2 in the wider smart-glasses market in 2025 (company-cited).\n- **Europe / Switzerland:** Europe is a sales market (online store and retail). **Swiss office, Swiss partners or Swiss pilots: none found / not public.** AURA is not yet sold in Europe.\n- **Partners:** Google (Android XR, Gemini), Qualcomm (Snapdragon Reality Elite), ASUS ROG (R1), Bose (audio), Unity (engine support), GameSir (controller), Sony (micro-OLED panels).\n- **Funding:** about **USD 433M** total; Jan 2026 round USD 100M at USD 1B; investors include unnamed supply-chain partners; earlier backer Pudong Venture Capital.\n- **Risks the company itself names:** US export controls, tariffs and trade policy; it says it is diversifying supply chain and channels.\n\n## Research and open-source activity\n\n| Area | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| GitHub ([nreal-ai](https://github.com/nreal-ai)) | **7 public repos**, mostly legacy: NRSDK-MRTK-Samples (58 stars, 2023), MRTK and WebXR forks. Only recent activity: `uvc_android` (\"uvc test with puck\", Jun 2026). The SDK is a closed tarball download |\n| SDK cadence | 3.0.0 → **3.1.0 (25 Nov 2025)**; no newer release visible in the docs as of today |\n| In-house R&D | Spatial co-processors **X1 / X1S**, optics (prism-style see-through, 70° FOV on AURA), SLAM / 6DoF tracking |\n| Academic output by XREAL | None found for robotics or embodied AI |\n| Third-party research using XREAL | \"ActiveGlasses\" (arXiv 2604.08534, Apr 2026) — egocentric demonstration capture with smart glasses for manipulation learning; academic, not an XREAL project |\n| Open standards | AURA sits on OpenXR via Android XR; XREAL SDK plugs into Unity AR Foundation / XR Hands rather than exposing OpenXR directly |\n| Models / datasets | None released |\n\nTakeaway: XREAL is a hardware and silicon company with a thin open-source footprint. Anything robotics-related you build is new ground for them.\n\n## What would impress this company at the hackathon\n\n**Inference — not stated by XREAL.** Based on its strategy (AI on glasses, Android XR ecosystem growth, consumer comfort, looking for use cases beyond video and gaming).\n\nWhat likely lands well in general:\n\n- A demo where the glasses are **worn for the whole task**, hands free — showing why light see-through glasses beat a VR headset or a tablet.\n- Use of what is **new**: Eye-based 6DoF + RGB camera on One series, or hands / Gemini on AURA.\n- A **non-entertainment use case** with a clear user — XREAL has none in public today.\n- Clean engineering on their stack that they could show to other developers (small open-source bridge, e.g. \"XREAL head pose → ROS 2\").\n\n| Track | Idea that fits XREAL's direction | Pairs with |\n|---|---|---|\n| **T01 Autonomous Urban Services** | One supervisor wearing glasses oversees several delivery / maintenance robots: world-fixed status cards, glance-to-select, takeover view from the robot camera | Quadrupeds or wheeled mobile manipulators (Unitree, AGIBOT, Galaxea) |\n| **T02 Resilient & Safe City** | Hands-free incident HUD for a mountain-rescue or infrastructure operator: quadruped camera feed + map in the glasses, **head pose steers the robot camera**, first-person view streamed to a remote expert | Go2 / B2-class quadruped; SKYLANDX scans as the map layer |\n| **T03 Sustainable City** | See-through guidance for sorting or inspection: robot-detected item classes or sensor readings shown in the worker's view; glasses RGB frames feed the classifier | Cobot arm vendors (Dobot, ROKAE, Flexiv) |\n| **T04 Elderly Care & Rehabilitation** | Therapist glasses showing live joint angles / force from a rehab robot while keeping eye contact with the patient; or a caregiver \"see what I see\" assist using Eye first-person streaming — strong Leukerbad clinic relevance | Force-controlled arms (Flexiv, ROKAE), OriginFlow EMG as an input signal |\n\nBonus points (inference): an **egocentric data-collection rig** (glasses RGB + 6DoF head pose, logged for robot learning) — it connects XREAL to the embodied-AI data trend that the other vendors on site care about.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [GamesBeat — XREAL raises USD 100M (15 Jan 2026)](https://gamesbeat.com/xreal-raises-100m-to-accelerate-ar-glasses-rollout/)\n- [UC Today — XREAL files for Hong Kong IPO (10 Apr 2026)](https://www.uctoday.com/devices-workspace-tech/xreal-files-for-hong-kong-ipo-as-smart-glasses-race-intensifies/)\n- [Caixin Global — XREAL founder interview (3 Jun 2026)](https://www.caixinglobal.com/2026-06-03/xreal-founder-bets-ai-will-push-smart-glasses-beyond-hype-102450675.html)\n- [Road to VR — XREAL AURA launch and pricing (28 Oct 2026)](https://www.roadtovr.com/google-ar-glasses-xreal-aura-release-date-price/)\n- [Unity blog — Unity support for XREAL AURA (16 Jun 2026)](https://unity.com/blog/unity-android-xr-wired-glasses-support)\n- [XREAL docs — SDK 3.1.0 release note](https://docs.xreal.com/Release%20Note/XREAL%20SDK%203.1.0)\n- [XREAL docs — Compatibility](https://docs.xreal.com/XREALDevices/Compatibility)\n- [GitHub — nreal-ai organisation](https://github.com/nreal-ai)\n- [arXiv 2604.08534 — ActiveGlasses](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.08534)\n- [XREAL profile in this vault (further sources listed there)](XREAL.md)\n","XREAL":"---\ntype: company\ncompany: \"XREAL\"\nslug: XREAL\nhq: \"Beijing, China\"\nfounded: 2017\nwebsite: https://www.xreal.com\ncategories: [xr, sensors, software]\non_site_guess: \"One-series AR glasses (One Pro / 1 Pro+ / 1S) with XREAL Eye camera and Beam Pro host; possibly AURA Android XR units and legacy Air 2 Ultra dev glasses\"\nfit_t01: 2\nfit_t02: 3\nfit_t03: 2\nfit_t04: 3\nidentity_confidence: high\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, company]\n---\n# XREAL\n\n> **One-liner:** XREAL (formerly Nreal) makes lightweight tethered AR glasses — a see-through wearable display with head tracking, plus (on some models) 6DoF, hand tracking and an Android XR compute puck.\n> **Why it matters at Leukerbad:** XREAL is not a robot — it is the **human interface layer**: an operator HUD, a hands-free instruction overlay for a patient or technician, or a head-pose / hand-pose input device for teleoperation. Pair it with another vendor's robot.\n\n## Snapshot\n\n| Item | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| HQ | Beijing, China (per trade press; the official About page does not state HQ) |\n| Founded | **2017** as Nreal; renamed XREAL in 2023 |\n| Founders / CEO | Founder and CEO Dr. Chi Xu (Xu Chi), with co-founders |\n| Size and funding | Headcount: not public. **USD 100M** round announced Jan 2026; about **USD 433M** raised in total (Crunchbase figure via GamesBeat). Reported valuation USD 0.83–1B ⚠️ unverified. 2025 revenue about **RMB 516M** (+30.8% YoY, >70% overseas) per IPO-filing coverage. HKEX main-board listing application filed **1 Apr 2026**; listing outcome not confirmed |\n| Core business | Consumer AR / display glasses, own spatial chips (X1, X1S), Android XR glasses with Google. Claims No. 1 in global AR-glasses revenue 2022–2025 (iResearch, company-cited) |\n| Website | https://www.xreal.com |\n| Docs / developer portal | https://docs.xreal.com (XREAL SDK) · https://developer.xreal.com (downloads) · Android XR docs for AURA: https://developer.android.com/develop/xr |\n| GitHub | https://github.com/nreal-ai (legacy Nreal-named org, linked from the official docs) |\n\n## Portfolio at a glance\n\n| Product | Type | Status | Key specs (verified unless marked) | Price (if public) | Img key |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| XREAL AURA (ex Project Aura) | Android XR optical see-through glasses + tethered compute puck | ✅ launched 28 Oct 2026; first batch ships mid-Nov 2026 (US, CA, JP, KR — **Europe later**) | **70° FOV** · 1920×1200 per eye, up to 120 Hz · **6DoF inside-out + hand tracking** · AI camera · glasses **<95 g** · puck: Snapdragon Reality Elite + X1S, 4,455 mAh · Gemini built in | **USD 1,279** (12/256) · USD 1,499 (16/512); launch promo USD 1,099 / 1,299 to Dec 2026 | xreal-aura |\n| XREAL 1 Pro+ | Tethered AR display glasses (flagship One series) | ✅ since 15 Oct 2026 | **57° FOV** · **1,200 nits** peak · X1 chip, native 3DoF · 60 Hz 2D-to-3D conversion · weight not published | **USD 599** | xreal-1-pro-plus |\n| XREAL One Pro | Tethered AR display glasses | ✅ (still listed; superseded by 1 Pro+) | **57° FOV** · 700 nits · X1 chip, native 3DoF, about 3 ms motion-to-photon · **87 g** · 6DoF only with XREAL Eye | USD 599 (launched at 649) | xreal-one-pro |\n| XREAL 1S | Tethered AR display glasses (entry One series) | ✅ since Jan 2026 | 1920×1200 (16:10) · X1 chip, native 3DoF · about **82 g** · FOV ⚠️ unverified | **USD 449** (seen at 399 on sale) | xreal-1s |\n| ROG XREAL R1 | Gaming AR glasses (with ASUS ROG) | ✅ pre-orders opened 2026; ship date ⚠️ unverified | **240 Hz** dual 1920×1080 micro-OLED · **57° FOV** · 91 g | about **USD 849** | xreal-rog-r1 |\n| XREAL Eye | Clip-in camera module for One series | ✅ | Adds **6DoF** tracking to One-series glasses (SDK 3.1.0+) · POV photo / video · sensor resolution ⚠️ unverified | **USD 99** | xreal-eye |\n| XREAL Beam Pro | Android handheld host / spatial camera (phone-like) | ✅ | Runs MyGlasses + XREAL SDK apps · one of the two officially tested SDK hosts · dual cameras for spatial capture | from **USD 199** | xreal-beam-pro |\n| XREAL Neo | Battery + video hub accessory | ✅ since Feb 2026 | Power bank and display hub for the glasses (e.g. handheld consoles) | USD 119 (99 launch) | xreal-neo |\n| xbx a01+ | Budget sub-brand display glasses | ✅ listed in site navigation; details ⚠️ unverified | Sub-brand \"XBX\" reported to start with a USD 299 A01; \"a01+\" specs not confirmed | not confirmed | xreal-xbx-a01-plus |\n| XREAL Air 2 Ultra | Developer-oriented 6DoF AR glasses (legacy) | ✅ shipped Mar 2024; no longer in main site navigation | Native **6DoF** (two SLAM cameras) + hand tracking · the SDK \"6DoF glasses\" reference device | USD 699 at launch | xreal-air-2-ultra |\n\nQuick capability map for builders:\n\n| Need | Works on |\n|---|---|\n| Just a big private screen / HUD (USB-C DisplayPort) | Every model — plug into laptop, phone, Jetson-class board with DP Alt Mode ⚠️ check your host port |\n| 3DoF head-locked / world-anchored screen without software | One Pro, 1 Pro+, 1S, R1 (X1 chip on glasses) |\n| 6DoF + hand tracking + planes / anchors via XREAL SDK | Air 2 Ultra; One series **with XREAL Eye** (6DoF confirmed; hand-tracking support on One + Eye ⚠️ unverified) |\n| Standalone Android XR apps, Gemini, Play Store | AURA only |\n\n## What they will most likely bring to the hackathon\n\n**Best guess (not confirmed):**\n\n- **One-series glasses (One Pro / 1 Pro+ / 1S) + XREAL Eye + Beam Pro** — the volume products, cheap to hand out, and the combination the current SDK (3.1.0) explicitly targets for 6DoF.\n- **A few AURA units** are plausible because it launched two weeks ago and XREAL / Google run an Android XR developer early-access programme — but AURA is not yet sold in Europe, so expect limited numbers if any.\n- **Air 2 Ultra** may appear as the classic developer device (native 6DoF + hand tracking without add-ons).\n- Ask at the booth first: (1) which glasses, (2) which host device (Beam Pro or Samsung S24 / S25 class phone), (3) whether camera frames are accessible, (4) whether a ready Unity sample is provided.\n\n## Developer platform (summary)\n\n- **XREAL SDK 3.1.0** (released 25 Nov 2025) — a **Unity** package (Unity 2021.3+), built on Unity XR Plugin, **AR Foundation**, **XR Interaction Toolkit** and **XR Hands**; replaces the older proprietary NRSDK (2.x). C# only; no official Unreal, native C++ or Python SDK found.\n- **Features:** 6DoF tracking (two SLAM cameras + IMU), plane detection, image tracking, hand tracking (**26 joints**, OpenXR-aligned), depth mesh, spatial anchors (persistent and shareable), phone-as-3DoF-controller. MRTK3 integration is documented.\n- **Hosts:** apps run on an **Android host** — officially tested on **Beam Pro and Samsung S25** (S24-and-later flagships listed). Docs warn **not to upgrade dev devices to Android 16** (compatibility issues under investigation).\n- **AURA is a different stack:** Android XR. Unity 6.5 with the Android XR OpenXR package, XR Hands, AR Foundation; hand tracking, planes, anchors, marker tracking and puck-trackpad input supported; **no eye tracking**. Standard Android apps also run from the Play Store.\n- **No-code path:** every model works as a plain USB-C external display — the fastest 48-hour route is to render your operator UI on a laptop / robot computer and show it in the glasses.\n- **ROS 2:** no official ROS 2 package found. Realistic path: Unity app + a Unity-to-ROS bridge (e.g. ROS-TCP-Connector or a WebSocket / UDP pose stream) publishing head and hand poses. Community XR-teleop projects exist but target Quest / Pico, not XREAL.\n- **Camera access caveat:** a 2026 research paper (ActiveGlasses) reports that XREAL did not expose raw camera frames on Air 2 Ultra, so the authors mounted a separate stereo camera. Whether XREAL Eye / AURA frames are available to apps: ⚠️ unverified — check on site.\n- **Simulation assets / datasets / foundation models:** none published by XREAL for robotics. AURA ships Gemini on-device as the assistant.\n- **On-board compute:** One-series glasses have only the X1 display / tracking co-processor (no app compute). AURA puck: Snapdragon Reality Elite (up to 48 TOPS).\n- Links: [docs](https://docs.xreal.com/) · [SDK downloads](https://developer.xreal.com/download) · [GitHub](https://github.com/nreal-ai) · [Android XR + Unity](https://developer.android.com/develop/xr/unity)\n\n## Current projects, deployments and customers\n\n- **2026-10-28** — XREAL AURA, the first Android XR optical see-through glasses, launched; first batch ships mid-November in US, Canada, Japan, South Korea; 150+ XR apps at launch ([Road to VR](https://www.roadtovr.com/google-ar-glasses-xreal-aura-release-date-price/)).\n- **2026-10-15** — XREAL 1 Pro+ on sale at USD 599 ([Road to VR](https://roadtovr.com/xreal-1-pro-plus-release-date-price/), [Android Authority](https://androidauthority.com/xreal-1-pro-plus-ar-glasses-launched-3723563)).\n- **2026-06-16** — Unity announced AURA support (Unity 6.5, Android XR packages) ([Unity blog](https://unity.com/blog/unity-android-xr-wired-glasses-support)).\n- **2026-05-19** — Google I/O: Android XR developer early-access programme for Project Aura hardware ([9to5Google](https://9to5google.com/2026/05/19/xreal-project-aura-android-xr-developers-2026-launch/)).\n- **2026-04** — Research use in robotics: \"ActiveGlasses\" (arXiv 2604.08534) uses XREAL Air 2 Ultra 6DoF head pose plus a stereo camera to collect egocentric manipulation demonstrations and replay active vision on a 6-DoF arm ([arXiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.08534)). This is academic work, not an XREAL product.\n- **2026-01 (CES)** — XREAL 1S, Neo hub and the ASUS co-developed ROG XREAL R1 announced ([Android Central](https://www.androidcentral.com/gaming/virtual-reality/xreal-1s-brings-1200p-visuals-and-a-lower-price-to-the-best-ar-glasses), [Engadget](https://www.engadget.com/gaming/pc/asus-and-xreal-teamed-up-at-ces-to-make-gaming-smartglasses-with-two-important-upgrades-190500897.html)).\n- **Partners:** Google (Android XR), Qualcomm (AURA chipset), ASUS ROG (R1), Bose (audio), Unity.\n- **Track-relevant deployments** (city services, safety, sustainability, elderly care): none found in public sources. The business is consumer entertainment / productivity.\n\n## Roadmap and future direction\n\n- **2026-01** — USD 100M funding round, stated purpose: accelerate AR glasses rollout ([GamesBeat](https://gamesbeat.com/xreal-raises-100m-to-accelerate-ar-glasses-rollout/)).\n- **2026-04-01** — Hong Kong main-board IPO application (sponsors CICC, Citi); aims to be the first listed smart-glasses pure play. Listing completion not confirmed as of this writing ([UC Today](https://www.uctoday.com/devices-workspace-tech/xreal-files-for-hong-kong-ipo-as-smart-glasses-race-intensifies/)).\n- **Late 2026 → 2027** — 🧪 AURA European availability \"soon after\" the first wave; 2027 retail expansion incl. select Google Store locations (US, Japan).\n- **Strategy** — two lines: affordable display glasses with in-house spatial chips (One series, XBX budget brand) and a premium Android XR / Gemini line (AURA). CEO positions AI as the driver for smart glasses ([Caixin](https://www.caixinglobal.com/2026-06-03/xreal-founder-bets-ai-will-push-smart-glasses-beyond-hype-102450675.html)).\n- **Robotics-specific roadmap:** not public. No announced robotics SDK, teleoperation product or embodied-AI data programme.\n\n## Challenge fit\n\n| Track | Fit 0–5 | Why (one line) | Best products | Go deeper |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| T01 Autonomous Urban Services | 2 | Supporting role: fleet-supervisor HUD or remote-takeover view for a delivery / maintenance robot | One Pro / 1 Pro+ as display; AURA for standalone app | [T01 brief](T01-Autonomous-Urban-Services/XREAL-T01-1-Brief.md) |\n| T02 Resilient & Safe City | 3 | Hands-free operator view for a quadruped / drone in hazard response; head-pose camera control; remote-expert overlay | One series + Eye (6DoF), AURA | [T02 brief](T02-Resilient-and-Safe-City/XREAL-T02-1-Brief.md) |\n| T03 Sustainable City | 2 | Supporting role: AR guidance for sorting / inspection or visualising sensor data on site | One series as display; AURA | [T03 brief](T03-Sustainable-City/XREAL-T03-1-Brief.md) |\n| T04 Elderly Care & Rehabilitation | 3 | Light glasses (82–95 g) suit therapist or patient: exercise cues, robot-status overlay, caregiver teleassist of an assistive robot | 1S / One Pro + Eye, AURA (hand tracking) | [T04 brief](T04-Elderly-Care-and-Rehabilitation/XREAL-T04-1-Brief.md) |\n\nDecision note: choose XREAL as an **add-on**, never as the core platform. Fastest win = glasses as an external display for a UI you already have; budget at least half a day if you want 6DoF / hand tracking through Unity.\n\n## Related docs\n- [Product portfolio](XREAL-Products.md) · [Developer guide](XREAL-Developer-Guide.md) · [Roadmap and strategy](XREAL-Roadmap-and-Strategy.md)\n- [Start here — all companies and tracks](../../00-START-HERE.md)\n\n## Sources\n- [XREAL — About](https://www.xreal.com/about)\n- [XREAL developer docs (SDK 3.1.0)](https://docs.xreal.com/)\n- [XREAL SDK 3.1.0 release note](https://docs.xreal.com/Release%20Note/XREAL%20SDK%203.1.0)\n- [XREAL SDK 3.0.0 release note](https://docs.xreal.com/Release%20Note/XREAL%20SDK%203.0.0)\n- [XREAL docs — Hand Tracking](https://docs.xreal.com/Input%20and%20Interactions/Hand%20Tracking)\n- [XREAL developer downloads](https://developer.xreal.com/download)\n- [XREAL US store — all products](https://us.shop.xreal.com/collections/all)\n- [Road to VR — XREAL Aura launch and pricing (28 Oct 2026)](https://www.roadtovr.com/google-ar-glasses-xreal-aura-release-date-price/)\n- [9to5Google — XREAL Aura starts at USD 1,279](https://9to5google.com/2026/10/07/xreal-aura-with-android-xr-start-at-1279/)\n- [9to5Google — Project Aura developer programme (Google I/O 2026)](https://9to5google.com/2026/05/19/xreal-project-aura-android-xr-developers-2026-launch/)\n- [Unity blog — Unity support for XREAL AURA](https://unity.com/blog/unity-android-xr-wired-glasses-support)\n- [Android Developers — Develop with Unity for Android XR](https://developer.android.com/develop/xr/unity)\n- [Road to VR — XREAL 1 Pro+ launch](https://roadtovr.com/xreal-1-pro-plus-release-date-price/)\n- [Android Authority — XREAL 1 Pro+ launched](https://androidauthority.com/xreal-1-pro-plus-ar-glasses-launched-3723563)\n- [Mixed News — XREAL 1 Pro+ details](https://mixed-news.com/en/xreal-1-pro-plus-599-dollars-1200-nits-one-pro-still-listed/)\n- [Android Central — XREAL 1S](https://www.androidcentral.com/gaming/virtual-reality/xreal-1s-brings-1200p-visuals-and-a-lower-price-to-the-best-ar-glasses)\n- [Ubergizmo — XREAL 1S review and specs](https://www.ubergizmo.com/reviews/xreal-1s-review-specs-weight-price-and-how-it-compares-to-the-xreal-one)\n- [Engadget — ASUS and XREAL ROG R1 at CES 2026](https://www.engadget.com/gaming/pc/asus-and-xreal-teamed-up-at-ces-to-make-gaming-smartglasses-with-two-important-upgrades-190500897.html)\n- [ASUS ROG — ROG XREAL R1 specs](https://rog.asus.com/glasses/rog-xreal-r1/spec/)\n- [Road to VR — ROG XREAL R1 pre-orders](https://roadtovr.com/rog-xreal-r1-pre-orders-now-live-240hz-microoled-gaming-glasses-priced-at-850/)\n- [Tom's Hardware — XREAL One series and X1 chip](https://www.tomshardware.com/peripherals/wearable-tech/hands-on-xreal-one-series-ar-glasses-announced-with-custom-x1-spatial-chip-more-customization-and-wider-fov)\n- [GamesBeat — XREAL raises USD 100M](https://gamesbeat.com/xreal-raises-100m-to-accelerate-ar-glasses-rollout/)\n- [UC Today — XREAL files for Hong Kong IPO](https://www.uctoday.com/devices-workspace-tech/xreal-files-for-hong-kong-ipo-as-smart-glasses-race-intensifies/)\n- [Caixin Global — XREAL founder interview (Jun 2026)](https://www.caixinglobal.com/2026-06-03/xreal-founder-bets-ai-will-push-smart-glasses-beyond-hype-102450675.html)\n- [arXiv 2604.08534 — ActiveGlasses](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.08534)\n","XREAL-T01-1-Brief":"---\ntype: challenge-brief\ncompany: \"XREAL\"\nslug: XREAL\nchallenge: T01\nchallenge_title: \"Autonomous Urban Services\"\nfit: 2\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t01, brief]\n---\n# XREAL × T01 Autonomous Urban Services — Brief\n\n**Fit: 2/5** — Supporting role only: the glasses are the human-in-the-loop screen, head-pose input and POV camera for someone else's delivery or maintenance robot.\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| Best hardware / platform for this track | **One-series glasses (One Pro / 1 Pro+ / 1S) + XREAL Eye + Beam Pro** — plug-in HUD on any USB-C DisplayPort host; Unity SDK adds **6DoF head pose + RGB frames**. Air 2 Ultra or AURA only for world-anchored AR. |\n| Strongest proof point (real deployment or demo + date) | **None in urban services.** Nearest: academic ActiveGlasses paper (arXiv, Apr 2026) using smart glasses for robot data collection. |\n| Fastest credible 48-hour demo | **Supervisor HUD**: robot camera, map and status rendered on a laptop and shown in the glasses, so a walking operator supervises / takes over a delivery robot hands-free. No SDK needed. |\n| Biggest risk | Plumbing: **no ROS 2 package**, Unity + Android host only (Beam Pro, Samsung S24 / S25; **not Android 16**); One + Eye has **no planes, anchors, image or hand tracking**. |\n| Pairs well with (other vendors on site) | Unitree (Go2 / B2 delivery base) · Galaxea / Galbot / UniX AI (wheeled service robots) · D-Robotics (edge compute) · SKYLANDX (3D map for the HUD) |\n\n## Why\n- Autonomous robots in a real town still need **supervision and remote takeover**; glasses make that hands-free (87 g class, 57° FOV, up to 1,200 nits on 1 Pro+).\n- **Zero-code path**: every model is a USB-C external display.\n- One + Eye exposes **RGB frames (YUV_420_888)** and 6DoF pose — enough for \"look at it, dispatch a robot to it\".\n- Cheap add-on (glasses USD 449–599, Eye USD 99) that does not block the core robot build.\n\n## Not a fit for\n- Teams expecting a robot body, navigation or autonomy from this vendor.\n- World-locked AR on One-series hardware (needs Air 2 Ultra or AURA; on-site availability unconfirmed).\n- ROS-only teams without Unity / Android skills who want more than a screen.\n\n## Sources\n- [XREAL docs — Compatibility](https://docs.xreal.com/XREALDevices/Compatibility) · [Access RGB Camera](https://docs.xreal.com/Camera/Access%20RGB%20Camera) · [arXiv 2604.08534](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.08534) · [XREAL profile](../XREAL.md)\n\n⬆ [XREAL profile](../XREAL.md) · [Products and solutions](XREAL-T01-2-Products-and-Solutions.md) · [Playbook](XREAL-T01-3-Playbook.md) · [Track overview](../../../Challenges/T01-Autonomous-Urban-Services/T01-0-Track-Overview.md)\n","XREAL-T01-2-Products-and-Solutions":"---\ntype: challenge-products\ncompany: \"XREAL\"\nslug: XREAL\nchallenge: T01\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t01, products]\n---\n# XREAL × T01 — Products and Solutions\n\n⬆ [XREAL profile](../XREAL.md) · [Brief](XREAL-T01-1-Brief.md) · [Playbook](XREAL-T01-3-Playbook.md)\n\n**Bottom line:** XREAL sells AR glasses, not robots. For T01 it supplies the **operator side** of an urban-service robot: a hands-free HUD, a head-pose input, and (with XREAL Eye) a first-person camera feed. Robot, navigation and autonomy must come from another vendor.\n\nResearch note: live web search was unavailable while this track file was written (budget exhausted), so track-specific findings rest on the official XREAL developer docs, the Android XR docs and the company profile research. Nothing in those sources shows an XREAL urban-services deployment.\n\n## Relevant products (ranked for this track)\n\nFeature matrix from the official SDK compatibility page (this decides which glasses you ask for):\n\n| Capability (XREAL SDK) | One series + XREAL Eye | Air 2 Ultra | Plain One series (no Eye) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Head tracking | **6DoF** | **6DoF** | 3DoF (on-glasses X1 chip) |\n| App access to RGB camera frames | **Yes** (YUV_420_888) | No RGB camera (dual grayscale SLAM cameras) | No |\n| First-person view capture / cast | App **and reality** | App only | App only |\n| Plane / image tracking, depth mesh, spatial anchors | **No** | Yes | No |\n| Hand tracking | **No** | Yes | No |\n| Controller | Phone as 3DoF controller | Phone as 3DoF controller | Phone as 3DoF controller |\n\n### XREAL One Pro\n![XREAL One Pro](../assets/xreal-one-pro.jpg)\n\n**Role in T01:** default supervisor / remote-takeover HUD for a delivery or maintenance robot.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| FOV | **57°** |\n| Brightness | 700 nits |\n| Weight | **87 g** |\n| Tracking | Native 3DoF (X1 chip); **6DoF with XREAL Eye** |\n| Connection | USB-C DisplayPort (tethered; no on-board app compute, no battery) |\n| IP / weather rating | not public — treat as indoor / dry-weather gear |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ shipping · USD 599 (superseded by 1 Pro+ but still listed).\n- **Developer control:** as a plain display — anything your host renders. Through XREAL SDK (Unity, Android host): head pose; with Eye, 6DoF pose and camera frames.\n\n### XREAL 1 Pro+\n![XREAL 1 Pro+](../assets/xreal-1-pro-plus.jpg)\n\n**Role in T01:** same as One Pro, better choice **outdoors** (snow glare, daylight streets).\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| FOV | **57°** |\n| Brightness | **1,200 nits** peak |\n| Tracking | Native 3DoF (X1 chip) |\n| Weight | not published |\n| XREAL Eye / SDK 6DoF support | ⚠️ unverified for this model (launched after SDK 3.1.0) — ask on site |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ since 15 Oct 2026 · USD 599.\n- **Developer control:** USB-C display; SDK behaviour expected to match One series ⚠️ unverified.\n\n### XREAL Eye\n![XREAL Eye](../assets/xreal-eye.jpg)\n\n**Role in T01:** turns One-series glasses into a 6DoF tracked headset with an operator-POV camera — the piece that enables \"look at a problem, send a robot\".\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Adds | **6DoF head tracking** + RGB camera to One series |\n| Frame access in SDK | `GetYUVFormatTextures()` → Y / U / V textures, format **YUV_420_888** |\n| Capture | Photo, video (Low / Middle / High), blend of virtual + real, first-person cast |\n| Sensor resolution | ⚠️ unverified |\n| Does **not** add | planes, image tracking, anchors, depth mesh, hand tracking |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ shipping · USD 99.\n- **Developer control:** camera textures and 6DoF pose inside a Unity app on the Android host; ready sample scenes `RGBCamera`, `RGBCameraAndCapture`, `FirstPersonViewStreamingCast`.\n\n### XREAL Beam Pro\n![XREAL Beam Pro](../assets/xreal-beam-pro.jpg)\n\n**Role in T01:** the pocket Android computer that runs your Unity app and talks to the robot over Wi-Fi.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Function | Android host for MyGlasses + XREAL SDK apps |\n| SDK support | Officially supported host (alongside Samsung Galaxy S24 / S25) |\n| Caveat | Docs: **do not use Android 16** on dev devices |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ shipping · from USD 199.\n- **Developer control:** normal Android device — install APKs, open sockets / WebSockets to the robot network.\n\n### XREAL 1S\n![XREAL 1S](../assets/xreal-1s.jpg)\n\n**Role in T01:** budget HUD; same workflow as One Pro with a narrower view.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| FOV | **52°** (official docs device table) |\n| Resolution | 1920×1200 |\n| Weight | about 82 g |\n| Tracking | Native 3DoF; accepts the detachable RGB accessory (Eye) per docs |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ since Jan 2026 · USD 449.\n\n### XREAL Air 2 Ultra\n![XREAL Air 2 Ultra](../assets/xreal-air-2-ultra.jpg)\n\n**Role in T01:** the only XREAL-SDK device with **world-anchored AR** (image tracking to localise against a robot marker, anchors, planes, hands) — needed for drawing a robot's path or drop-off point in the real street / corridor.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Tracking | Native **6DoF**, dual grayscale cameras |\n| AR features | Planes, image tracking, hand tracking (26 joints), depth mesh, spatial anchors |\n| RGB camera | None |\n| FOV | Docs are inconsistent (52° in text, 46° in table) |\n| Anchor guidance | Keep content within **3 m** of an anchor; needs textured, evenly lit surroundings |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ legacy (shipped Mar 2024, USD 699 at launch); availability on site unconfirmed.\n\n### XREAL AURA\n![XREAL AURA](../assets/xreal-aura.jpg)\n\n**Role in T01:** stretch option — standalone Android XR glasses with compute puck; could run the whole operator app (and Gemini) without a phone.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| FOV | **70°** |\n| Tracking | 6DoF inside-out + hand tracking |\n| Compute | Puck with Snapdragon Reality Elite + X1S, 4,455 mAh |\n| Weight (glasses) | <95 g |\n| Dev stack | Android XR: Unity 6 + Unity OpenXR Android XR package + Android XR Extensions (planes, persistent / shareable anchors, hands; Geospatial VPS listed for the platform — per-device support on AURA ⚠️ unverified) |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ launched 28 Oct 2026, first batch mid-Nov, **US / CA / JP / KR only** — on-site units unlikely. USD 1,279 / 1,499.\n\nNot relevant for T01: ROG XREAL R1 (gaming display), XREAL Neo (battery / hub — useful only as a power accessory), xbx a01+ (details unverified).\n\n## What XREAL already does in this domain\n\n- **Nothing public.** No deployment, pilot, demo, partnership or application page for delivery, cleaning, maintenance, concierge or wayfinding robots was found. The business is consumer entertainment / productivity glasses.\n- **Nearest robotics evidence (academic, not XREAL):** *ActiveGlasses* (arXiv 2604.08534, submitted 9 Apr 2026) — smart glasses worn by a demonstrator, then the same camera mounted on a 6-DoF perception arm for policy replay. The company profile research identifies the glasses as XREAL Air 2 Ultra used for head pose, with a separate stereo camera because raw frames were not exposed on that model.\n- **No official ROS 2 package, robotics SDK, teleoperation product, sim assets or datasets.**\n\n## What they are building next in this domain\n\n- **Announced:** AURA / Android XR roll-out (Europe \"later\"), Gemini built in. No robotics or urban-services roadmap is public.\n- **Inference (not announced):** an Android XR device with Gemini, cameras and (platform-level) geospatial anchoring is a natural base for pedestrian wayfinding and field-worker assistance; that is adjacent to T01 but is not a robot product and nothing ties XREAL to city-service robotics.\n\n## Software, models and data that help for this track\n\n| Asset | What it gives you in 48 h | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| USB-C DisplayPort mode | Any existing UI (RViz, Foxglove, web dashboard, video) in the glasses with zero code | Host port must support DP Alt Mode ⚠️ check robot computers / dev boards |\n| XREAL SDK 3.1.0 (Unity 2021.3+, AR Foundation / XRI) | 6DoF head pose, camera frames (Eye), phone-as-controller | C# only; Android host |\n| SDK samples | `HelloMR`, `RGBCamera`, `RGBCameraAndCapture`, `FirstPersonViewStreamingCast`, `BasicImageTracking`, `Anchors`, `MarkerTracking` (experimental) | Image tracking / anchors only on Air 2 Ultra |\n| Unity ROS-TCP-Connector (Unity Technologies, Apache-2.0) | Publish head pose / subscribe to robot topics from the Unity app | Third-party to XREAL; README badges list ROS 2 Foxy / Galactic — newer distros ⚠️ unverified. Plain WebSocket / UDP JSON is the lower-risk alternative |\n| Android XR Unity packages (AURA only) | Planes, anchors, hands via AR Foundation / XR Hands | Only if AURA hardware is on site |\n| Models / datasets | none from XREAL | Bring your own VLM / detector |\n\n## Gaps and limits for this track\n\n- **Not a robot:** zero payload, mobility, manipulation or autonomy. Fit depends entirely on the partner robot.\n- **No ROS 2 bridge out of the box** — budget half a day for Unity ↔ robot plumbing.\n- **One + Eye is thinner than expected:** 6DoF pose and camera only; no hands, planes, anchors or image tracking. World-locked overlays need Air 2 Ultra or AURA.\n- **Tethered:** glasses need a host and cable; a walking operator carries a Beam Pro / phone or laptop.\n- **Outdoor use:** optical see-through washes out in bright snow; prefer 1 Pro+ (1,200 nits). No public IP rating; cold-weather behaviour not public.\n- **Tracking outdoors / over distance:** visual-inertial tracking and anchors are documented for near-field, textured, evenly lit scenes (0.5–3 m guidance) — do not promise street-scale AR registration.\n- **Host constraints:** Beam Pro or Samsung S24 / S25; avoid Android 16.\n- **On-site hardware is unconfirmed** — check the booth before committing to a design.\n\n## Sources\n- [XREAL developer docs — overview (SDK 3.1.0)](https://docs.xreal.com/)\n- [XREAL docs — Compatibility (feature matrix, supported phones)](https://docs.xreal.com/XREALDevices/Compatibility)\n- [XREAL docs — XREAL Glasses (device table)](https://docs.xreal.com/XREALDevices/XREAL%20Glasses)\n- [XREAL docs — Access RGB Camera](https://docs.xreal.com/Camera/Access%20RGB%20Camera)\n- [XREAL docs — Sample Code](https://docs.xreal.com/Sample%20Code)\n- [XREAL docs — Spatial Anchor intro](https://docs.xreal.com/Spatial%20Anchor/intro)\n- [Android Developers — Develop with Unity for Android XR](https://developer.android.com/develop/xr/unity)\n- [Unity-Technologies / ROS-TCP-Connector (GitHub)](https://github.com/Unity-Technologies/ROS-TCP-Connector)\n- [arXiv 2604.08534 — ActiveGlasses](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.08534)\n- [XREAL company profile (internal, prices and launch dates with their own sources)](../XREAL.md)\n","XREAL-T01-3-Playbook":"---\ntype: challenge-playbook\ncompany: \"XREAL\"\nslug: XREAL\nchallenge: T01\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t01, playbook]\n---\n# XREAL × T01 — Hackathon Playbook\n\n⬆ [XREAL profile](../XREAL.md) · [Brief](XREAL-T01-1-Brief.md) · [Products and solutions](XREAL-T01-2-Products-and-Solutions.md)\n\n**Read first:** XREAL is an add-on. Every concept below needs a robot from another vendor; the glasses provide the human-in-the-loop layer. Statements about other vendors' robots are indicative — check their own profiles. Confirm at the XREAL booth which glasses and hosts are actually available before choosing a concept.\n\n## Solution approaches\n\n### Concept 1 — Walk-along supervisor HUD (no SDK)\n- **Problem → value:** A delivery / luggage robot in a pedestrian village still needs a human minder. Today that person stares at a tablet. A HUD keeps eyes on the street and hands free → one minder can safely escort or supervise.\n- **Hardware:** One Pro or 1 Pro+ (outdoor: 1 Pro+) as USB-C display on a laptop in a backpack or a DP-capable phone. Robot: e.g. Unitree Go2 / B2 or a wheeled service robot (Galaxea, Galbot, UniX AI).\n- **Architecture / stack:** Robot runs its own navigation (vendor stack / ROS 2 Nav2) → publishes camera, pose, battery, mission state → a web dashboard (or Foxglove / RViz layout) on the host → mirrored into the glasses. Input by gamepad or phone. All compute on robot + host; glasses are a monitor.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: get glasses showing a laptop screen; get robot video + pose onto the network.\n  - H6–24: build one high-contrast dashboard (video, mini-map, next waypoint, E-stop state); robot runs a fixed A → B delivery route.\n  - H24–40: add takeover: gamepad teleop when the robot reports \"blocked\"; alert banner in HUD.\n  - H40–48: rehearse route, record backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (1) Robot starts hotel → clinic delivery autonomously; operator walks beside it wearing glasses. (2) An obstacle blocks the path; HUD flashes \"blocked\", operator takes over by gamepad without looking down. (3) Robot resumes autonomy and hands over the parcel; show the HUD view on the projector.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** Host port lacks DP Alt Mode → use a phone / laptop that has it or an HDMI adapter ⚠️ check. Glare outdoors → demo indoors or in shade. Fallback: same dashboard on a tablet.\n- **Judging angles:** Innovation low–medium · technical depth sits in the robot · impact: realistic operations model (supervised autonomy) · Leukerbad: car-light village, luggage and spa-supply runs between hotels and clinics.\n\n### Concept 2 — Head-steered remote takeover\n- **Problem → value:** When an autonomous robot gets stuck, a remote operator needs fast situational awareness. Turning the head to look around is quicker than joystick camera control → shorter recovery time per incident.\n- **Hardware:** One series + XREAL Eye + Beam Pro (6DoF), or plain One series (3DoF head rotation is enough). Robot with a pan-tilt camera, a 360° camera, or a quadruped whose body yaw / pitch can follow the head.\n- **Architecture / stack:** Unity app (XREAL SDK 3.1.0) on Beam Pro reads head pose → sends yaw / pitch at 30–60 Hz over UDP / WebSocket (or ROS-TCP-Connector) → robot-side node maps it to the camera gimbal or body orientation → low-latency video (WebRTC / MJPEG) back to a quad in the Unity scene. Driving via phone-as-controller or gamepad. Autonomy stays on the robot.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: build and deploy the SDK `HelloMR` sample; print head pose; confirm host + glasses combo.\n  - H6–24: pose stream to a robot-side script; video stream into Unity; measure glass-to-glass latency.\n  - H24–40: integrate with the robot's \"need help\" state; add reticle, latency and battery indicators.\n  - H40–48: tune smoothing / dead-zones; rehearse.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (1) Operator sits out of sight of the robot. (2) Robot on a service route stops at an ambiguous obstacle and calls for help. (3) Operator looks left / right — robot camera follows — picks a gap, drives through, releases control.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** Latency over venue Wi-Fi → bring your own router, lower resolution. Unity ↔ ROS friction → plain JSON over WebSocket. No Eye available → 3DoF rotation only (fine for this concept).\n- **Judging angles:** Innovation medium · technical depth: real-time loop, latency budget · impact: remote-ops cost per robot · Leukerbad: one operator at the tourist office covering robots across steep, snowy lanes.\n\n### Concept 3 — \"Look and dispatch\" for municipal maintenance\n- **Problem → value:** Faults in public space (overflowing bin, ice patch, blocked path, spill in a spa corridor) are noticed by staff on foot but reported late. Worker looks at the issue, says / clicks \"report\" → a robot is dispatched to handle or cordon it.\n- **Hardware:** One series + **XREAL Eye** + Beam Pro (RGB frames are confirmed accessible on this combo). Robot: quadruped or wheeled manipulator for the response. Optional: D-Robotics RDK board for on-robot perception; SKYLANDX scan as the shared site map.\n- **Architecture / stack:** Unity app grabs Eye frame (YUV → JPEG) + 6DoF pose on trigger → HTTP to a laptop service → VLM / detector classifies the issue and writes a task (type, photo, location) → task queue → robot navigates to the location and performs a simple action (stand guard with warning light, photograph, pick up item). Location: nearest named waypoint chosen by the worker in the HUD list, or a fiducial at the site — do **not** rely on glasses pose for town-scale coordinates. Result and robot ETA shown back in the HUD.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: run `RGBCameraAndCapture` sample; save one frame; choose 3 issue types and 4 waypoints.\n  - H6–24: frame upload + VLM classification; task queue; robot goes to named waypoints.\n  - H24–40: HUD feedback (task accepted, ETA, done photo); one robot response behaviour per issue type.\n  - H40–48: end-to-end runs, failure handling, backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (1) Worker walks past a tipped bin, looks at it, taps the phone controller. (2) Screen shows classification \"waste overflow, waypoint 3\"; robot leaves its dock. (3) Robot arrives, performs the response; HUD shows \"resolved\" with the robot's photo.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** Camera access or resolution weaker than expected → use the Beam Pro's own camera for the photo. VLM latency → small fixed-class detector. Robot navigation unreliable → shorten the route, pre-map.\n- **Judging angles:** Innovation medium–high (human perception + robot action) · technical depth: full perception → task → navigation loop · impact: faster municipal response with existing staff · Leukerbad: ice and snow hazards on walkways used by elderly spa guests.\n\n### Concept 4 — Shared-space AR: see the robot's intent (most ambitious)\n- **Problem → value:** People distrust service robots in crowded pedestrian areas because they cannot tell where the robot will go. Staff with glasses see the robot's planned path, drop-off point and stop zone drawn in the real corridor → smoother shared use of space and faster handovers.\n- **Hardware:** **Air 2 Ultra** (image tracking + anchors) or **AURA** (Android XR) — One + Eye cannot do this. Robot with a known planner output (Nav2 path). Printed image marker on the robot or at its dock.\n- **Architecture / stack:** Glasses detect the marker (SDK `BasicImageTracking` / experimental `MarkerTracking`) → compute the transform glasses-frame ↔ robot map frame → subscribe to path and goal topics → render path ribbon and goal disc in Unity. Re-localise each time the marker is seen; keep overlays within a few metres of the marker (docs advise ≤3 m from an anchor).\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: confirm device availability; run image-tracking sample; static cube locked to marker.\n  - H6–24: frame alignment maths; stream the robot path; draw it.\n  - H24–40: goal editing by gaze + phone controller (or hand pinch on Air 2 Ultra); send new goal to the robot.\n  - H40–48: stabilise drift, rehearse in the exact demo spot and lighting.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (1) Concierge wearing glasses sees a glowing path from the robot to room door B. (2) Guest changes the request; concierge drags the goal disc to a new spot; robot replans and the path updates live. (3) Robot arrives exactly on the disc.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** Hardware not on site → drop to Concept 2. Drift / misalignment → marker on the robot, short distances, indoor only. Fallback: show the same path on a top-down map in the HUD (Concept 1).\n- **Judging angles:** Innovation high · technical depth: co-localisation, bidirectional control · impact: human–robot trust in public space · Leukerbad: hotel and clinic corridors with slow-moving guests.\n\n## Cross-vendor combos worth considering\n\n| Combo | What it enables | Integration effort |\n|---|---|---|\n| XREAL One series + Unitree Go2 / B2 | Supervised delivery on steep or snowy paths with HUD and head-steered camera | Medium — video + pose stream, vendor SDK details per Unitree profile |\n| XREAL + Galaxea / Galbot / UniX AI wheeled manipulators | Hotel / clinic concierge robot with staff HUD for handover and takeover | Medium — depends on the robot's exposed topics |\n| XREAL Eye + D-Robotics RDK | Glasses frames or robot frames processed at the edge instead of a laptop | Medium — network + model deployment |\n| XREAL + SKYLANDX MetaCam | Pre-scanned 3D map of the venue as the mini-map / shared waypoint frame | Low–medium — export map, render top-down |\n| XREAL (Air 2 Ultra / AURA) + any Nav2 robot | Path and goal overlays in the real space (Concept 4) | High — co-localisation and device availability |\n| XREAL + Binabik AI / Genisom agent stacks | Natural-language tasking of the robot with HUD confirmation | Unknown — APIs not verified |\n\n## Questions to ask the XREAL engineers on site\n1. Which glasses, how many XREAL Eye modules and which hosts (Beam Pro, Samsung S24 / S25) can teams borrow — and are any Air 2 Ultra or AURA units here?\n2. Does 1 Pro+ work with XREAL Eye and SDK 3.1.0 for 6DoF, or only One / One Pro / 1S?\n3. What resolution and frame rate do Eye RGB frames reach inside a Unity app, and can they be streamed while 6DoF tracking runs?\n4. Is there a ready sample for sending head pose over the network (UDP / WebSocket / ROS)? Any internal ROS 2 bridge?\n5. How does 6DoF tracking behave outdoors — snow, low texture, bright light, walking 50+ m?\n6. Can the glasses run from a Linux laptop or a robot dev board as a plain display (DP Alt Mode / HDMI adapter), and is head pose readable on Linux without the Android SDK?\n7. Is `FirstPersonViewStreamingCast` usable for low-latency remote viewing, and over which protocol?\n8. Is a newer SDK than 3.1.0 or an on-site build available, and which Android versions must be avoided?\n\n## Decision rule\n- **Pick XREAL for T01 if** you already have a robot team-mate / vendor for mobility and autonomy and want a credible supervised-autonomy or remote-takeover story with a wearable HUD — starting with the display-only path and adding head pose or Eye camera only if time allows.\n- **Avoid if** you expected a robot platform, need world-anchored AR but only One-series glasses are on site, or nobody on the team can build a Unity Android app and you want more than a screen.\n\n## Sources\n- [XREAL developer docs — overview (SDK 3.1.0)](https://docs.xreal.com/)\n- [XREAL docs — Compatibility (feature matrix, supported phones)](https://docs.xreal.com/XREALDevices/Compatibility)\n- [XREAL docs — XREAL Glasses (device table)](https://docs.xreal.com/XREALDevices/XREAL%20Glasses)\n- [XREAL docs — Access RGB Camera](https://docs.xreal.com/Camera/Access%20RGB%20Camera)\n- [XREAL docs — Sample Code](https://docs.xreal.com/Sample%20Code)\n- [XREAL docs — Spatial Anchor intro](https://docs.xreal.com/Spatial%20Anchor/intro)\n- [Android Developers — Develop with Unity for Android XR](https://developer.android.com/develop/xr/unity)\n- [Unity-Technologies / ROS-TCP-Connector (GitHub)](https://github.com/Unity-Technologies/ROS-TCP-Connector)\n- [XREAL company profile (internal)](../XREAL.md)\n","XREAL-T02-1-Brief":"---\ntype: challenge-brief\ncompany: \"XREAL\"\nslug: XREAL\nchallenge: T02\nchallenge_title: \"Resilient & Safe City\"\nfit: 3\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t02, brief]\n---\n# XREAL × T02 Resilient & Safe City — Brief\n\n**Fit: 3/5** — A good hands-free operator interface (HUD, head-pose input, first-person video link) for someone else's robot; XREAL has no robot, no rugged device and no public safety deployments.\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| Best hardware / platform for this track | **One-series glasses (One Pro / 1 Pro+ / 1S) + XREAL Eye + Beam Pro**: plug-in HUD on any USB-C DisplayPort host; with the SDK, 6DoF head pose + RGB frames + first-person streaming. **Air 2 Ultra** or **AURA** if you need anchors / hand tracking |\n| Strongest proof point (real deployment or demo + date) | No XREAL safety / rescue / inspection deployment found. Closest: **ActiveGlasses** (arXiv, Apr 2026) — researchers used Air 2 Ultra 6DoF head pose to drive a camera-carrying robot arm (with a Flexiv Rizon4) |\n| Fastest credible 48-hour demo | Patrol quadruped streams camera + alerts to a laptop; operator sees it in the glasses as a **head-up console**, hands free for the gamepad. No XREAL SDK code |\n| Biggest risk | SDK path = Unity + Android host (Beam Pro / Samsung S24–S25, **not Android 16**). One + Eye gives **6DoF only** — no hand tracking, planes, anchors. Consumer hardware, cable-tethered, no IP rating found |\n| Pairs well with (other vendors on site) | Unitree Go2 / B2, AGIBOT D1, Magiclab MagicDog, Genisom M1 / L1 (scout robots) · SKYLANDX MetaCam (3D map) · D-Robotics RDK (edge video) · Flexiv (head-driven camera arm) |\n\n## Why\n- **Hands-free situational awareness** is a real responder need: watch the robot's feed without looking down at a tablet.\n- **Lowest integration cost on site**: every model is a USB-C external display — your existing web / RViz UI just appears in the glasses.\n- **Head pose as input**: 6DoF via Eye or Air 2 Ultra — look around to steer a robot's camera.\n- **Documented first-person streaming** (One + Eye → Windows receiver on the same LAN) gives a command-post \"see what I see\" link.\n- **1 Pro+ at 1,200 nits** is the most usable model on snow / outdoors; glasses weigh **82–95 g**.\n\n## Not a fit for\n- Teams wanting XREAL as the **core platform** — it cannot sense hazards, move or act.\n- Claims about **rugged, gloved, wet or sub-zero** field use — nothing public supports them.\n- Teams with **no Unity / Android skills** who still want 6DoF or anchors in 48 hours.\n\n⬆ [XREAL profile](../XREAL.md) · [Products and solutions](XREAL-T02-2-Products-and-Solutions.md) · [Playbook](XREAL-T02-3-Playbook.md) · [Track overview](../../../Challenges/T02-Resilient-and-Safe-City/T02-0-Track-Overview.md)\n","XREAL-T02-2-Products-and-Solutions":"---\ntype: challenge-products\ncompany: \"XREAL\"\nslug: XREAL\nchallenge: T02\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t02, products]\n---\n# XREAL × T02 — Products and Solutions\n\n⬆ [XREAL profile](../XREAL.md) · [Brief](XREAL-T02-1-Brief.md) · [Playbook](XREAL-T02-3-Playbook.md)\n\n**Read this first:** XREAL sells AR glasses, not robots. In T02 its role is the **operator / responder interface** on top of another vendor's robot. Which glasses you get decides what you can build:\n\n| You need | Device that does it (per official SDK compatibility table) |\n|---|---|\n| Head-up screen only, no code | Any model on a USB-C DisplayPort host |\n| 6DoF head pose + **RGB camera frames** + first-person view with real-world video | **One series + XREAL Eye** |\n| 6DoF + hand tracking + planes + image tracking + depth mesh + spatial anchors | **Air 2 Ultra** (no RGB camera) |\n| All spatial features + standalone compute + Gemini | **AURA** (Android XR stack) |\n\nThe One series + Eye column in XREAL's own table reads **\"No\"** for plane tracking, image tracking, hand tracking, depth mesh and spatial anchors. Do not plan on those with One + Eye.\n\n## Relevant products (ranked for this track)\n\n### XREAL One Pro\n\n![XREAL One Pro](../assets/xreal-one-pro.jpg)\n\n**Role in T02:** default operator HUD. Shows a robot's video, map and alerts while the operator's hands stay on a controller; with XREAL Eye it also becomes a 6DoF head-pose input device.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Field of view | **57°** |\n| Brightness | 700 nits |\n| Tracking | Native 3DoF on the X1 chip (about 3 ms motion-to-photon); **6DoF only with XREAL Eye** |\n| Weight | **87 g** |\n| Connection | USB-C DisplayPort, cable-tethered |\n| Ruggedness / IP rating | not public |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping · USD 599 (superseded by 1 Pro+, still listed).\n- **Developer control:** as a display — nothing to program. Via XREAL SDK 3.1.0 (Unity, Android host): head pose, 3DoF phone controller, rendering.\n\n### XREAL 1 Pro+\n\n![XREAL 1 Pro+](../assets/xreal-1-pro-plus.jpg)\n\n**Role in T02:** same as One Pro, but the pick for **outdoor / snow-glare** use thanks to peak brightness.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Field of view | **57°** |\n| Brightness | **1,200 nits** peak |\n| Tracking | Native 3DoF (X1 chip) |\n| Weight | not published |\n| XREAL Eye / SDK 6DoF support | ⚠️ unverified (SDK 3.1.0 predates this model — ask on site) |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping since 15 Oct 2026 · **USD 599**.\n- **Developer control:** display path certain; SDK path to be confirmed.\n\n### XREAL Eye\n\n![XREAL Eye](../assets/xreal-eye.jpg)\n\n**Role in T02:** the part that turns One-series display glasses into a sensor. It provides the 6DoF upgrade and is the **only XREAL camera whose RGB frames the SDK documents as readable**.\n\n| Capability | Detail (official docs) |\n|---|---|\n| Head tracking with One series | **6DoF** |\n| RGB frame access | Yes — YUV_420_888 textures in Unity (RGBCamera sample) |\n| Photo / video capture | Yes — blend modes: virtual + real, RGB only, virtual only |\n| First-person view streaming | Yes, with real-world video — to a Windows receiver app on the same LAN |\n| Resolution / frame rate | not stated in docs ⚠️ unverified |\n| Hand tracking, planes, image tracking, mesh, anchors | **No** on One + Eye |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping · **USD 99**.\n- **Developer control:** camera textures, recording and streaming prefabs in Unity. Frames arrive on the Android host — forwarding them to a robot stack is your own code.\n\n### XREAL Beam Pro\n\n![XREAL Beam Pro](../assets/xreal-beam-pro.jpg)\n\n**Role in T02:** the pocket Android host that runs your Unity app, so the responder is untethered from a laptop.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Function | Android handheld; one of the officially supported SDK hosts (the other: Samsung Galaxy S24 / S25) |\n| Networking for robot link | Wi-Fi (your app talks to the robot over LAN) |\n| Android version | Keep below **Android 16** (docs warn of unresolved compatibility issues) |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping · from **USD 199**.\n- **Developer control:** standard Android + Unity app deployment (adb).\n\n### XREAL Air 2 Ultra\n\n![XREAL Air 2 Ultra](../assets/xreal-air-2-ultra.jpg)\n\n**Role in T02:** the only non-AURA device with the full spatial feature set — needed for **world-anchored hazard markers**, marker-based alignment to a robot map, or hand-gesture commands.\n\n| Capability | Detail (official docs) |\n|---|---|\n| Head tracking | **6DoF** (dual grayscale cameras) |\n| Plane / image tracking, hand tracking (26 joints), depth mesh, spatial anchors | **Yes** |\n| RGB camera | **No** |\n| Grayscale camera frames to apps | Not exposed, according to the ActiveGlasses authors (Apr 2026) |\n| First-person view | Virtual content only |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipped Mar 2024, legacy (USD 699 at launch); on-site availability not confirmed.\n- **Developer control:** XREAL SDK 3.1.0 through AR Foundation / XR Hands on Beam Pro or Samsung S24 / S25.\n\n### XREAL AURA\n\n![XREAL AURA](../assets/xreal-aura.jpg)\n\n**Role in T02:** highest-capability option — standalone compute puck, widest view, hands as input — if units are actually at the venue.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Field of view | **70°** |\n| Tracking / input | 6DoF inside-out + hand tracking; puck trackpad |\n| Compute | Puck with Snapdragon Reality Elite + X1S, 4,455 mAh battery |\n| Weight (glasses) | **<95 g** |\n| AI | Gemini built in |\n| Android XR Unity features (platform level) | Planes, persistent anchors, occlusion / depth, object tracking, hand tracking; per-device support on AURA ⚠️ unverified |\n| App access to camera frames | ⚠️ unverified |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ launched 28 Oct 2026, first batch ships mid-Nov; **USD 1,279 / 1,499**; US, Canada, Japan, South Korea only — **not yet sold in Europe**.\n- **Developer control:** Android XR — Unity 6 with the Unity OpenXR: Android XR package and Android XR Extensions; a different code base from XREAL SDK 3.x.\n\n### XREAL 1S\n\n![XREAL 1S](../assets/xreal-1s.jpg)\n\n**Role in T02:** cheapest HUD; hand several to a \"command post\" team.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Display | 1920×1200 per eye; FOV ⚠️ unverified |\n| Tracking | Native 3DoF; docs say One-series glasses take the Eye accessory, 1S not named individually ⚠️ unverified |\n| Weight | about **82 g** |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping · **USD 449**.\n\n### XREAL Neo\n\n![XREAL Neo](../assets/xreal-neo.jpg)\n\n**Role in T02:** minor — battery + video hub that can keep glasses powered away from a desk. Capacity and suitability as a field power source: ⚠️ unverified. ✅ shipping · USD 119.\n\n**Skip for this track:** ROG XREAL R1 (gaming, 240 Hz) and xbx a01+ (budget display) add nothing here beyond being a screen.\n\n## What XREAL already does in this domain\n\n- **Nothing public.** The company profile research found no deployments, pilots, demos, partner programmes or application pages for emergency response, inspection, security or teleoperation. XREAL's business is consumer entertainment and productivity.\n- **Caveat on this statement:** the track-specific web search could not be run for this document (search quota exhausted); the finding rests on the profile research plus official docs read today. Absence of evidence, not proof of absence.\n- **Closest third-party evidence (academic, not an XREAL project):**\n  - **2026-04** — *ActiveGlasses* (arXiv 2604.08534): Air 2 Ultra's 6DoF tracking records the wearer's head motion; a ZED Mini stereo camera is mounted on the glasses because XREAL does not let apps call the cameras directly; a perception arm then reproduces the head motion while a **Flexiv Rizon4** manipulates. Relevant as proof that XREAL head pose is stable enough to drive a robot's viewpoint ([arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.08534)).\n\n## What they are building next in this domain\n\n- **Announced, general:** AURA rollout to Europe \"soon after\" the first wave 🧪; Android XR + Gemini as the premium line; cheaper display glasses with in-house chips (see [profile](../XREAL.md)).\n- **Safety / robotics specific:** not public. No announced enterprise, first-responder, teleoperation or robotics SDK programme.\n- **Inference (ours, not XREAL's):** Android XR brings platform features such as geospatial anchors (VPS), object tracking and on-device Gemini that suit field guidance and \"what am I looking at\" assistance. Whether XREAL pursues safety / industrial customers is unknown.\n\n## Software, models and data that help for this track\n\n| Asset | What it gives you | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **XREAL SDK 3.1.0** (Unity package) | Head pose, AR Foundation features, XR Interaction Toolkit rigs | Unity 2021.3+; Android host only |\n| Sample: **RGBCamera / RGBCameraAndCapture** | Read Eye RGB frames; record video / photos | Starting point for POV capture or running detection on the wearer's view |\n| Sample: **FirstPersonViewStreamingCast** + StreamingReceiver (Windows) | One-tap POV stream to a PC on the same LAN | Docs advise a dedicated Wi-Fi / hotspot and firewall off; protocol not documented |\n| Samples: **Spatial Anchor**, **Image Tracking**, **XREAL Marker**, **Plane Detection**, **Basic Meshing** | World-locked content, marker-based alignment | Air 2 Ultra only (per compatibility table); marker sample device support ⚠️ unverified |\n| **Simulator** (Unity Editor) | Develop without glasses on your head | Useful when the team shares one device |\n| **CloudXR / XR Streaming** tool | Streams SteamVR content from a PC to the glasses over Wi-Fi | Possible route to PC-rendered 3D scenes; latency untested |\n| **MRTK3 integration** | Ready-made UI controls | Saves UI time |\n| **Android XR Unity packages** (AURA) | Planes, persistent anchors, occlusion, hand tracking via XR Hands, geospatial | Unity 6, URP + Vulkan recommended |\n| GitHub org `nreal-ai` | MRTK fork, older NRSDK samples, a UVC test project | Mostly legacy; **no ROS 2 code** |\n\n- **Robotics bridge:** none official. Use Unity's ROS-TCP-Connector or a plain UDP / WebSocket pose stream to a small ROS 2 node.\n- **Models / datasets / simulation assets for safety or inspection:** none from XREAL.\n\n## Gaps and limits for this track\n\n- **Not a robot:** no mobility, no hazard sensing, no actuation. Every T02 concept needs another vendor's platform.\n- **Feature split across devices:** RGB camera only on One + Eye; anchors / hand tracking only on Air 2 Ultra or AURA. No single 3.x device has both.\n- **Camera access is narrow:** Eye RGB only. No thermal, no depth stream, SLAM cameras not exposed.\n- **Field hardness:** consumer glasses; no IP rating, operating-temperature range or helmet / glove compatibility found. Cable to host. Optical see-through washes out in bright snow (1 Pro+ mitigates).\n- **Stack lock-in:** Unity + Android only; no Python, C++, ROS 2 or web SDK. Android 16 hosts are discouraged.\n- **Head pose on a laptop host:** the display path gives a screen only; reading head pose from a PC is not documented ⚠️ unverified.\n- **AURA availability:** launched two weeks ago, not sold in Europe; do not rely on it until you see units.\n- **No domain track record:** nothing to cite to judges as an XREAL safety deployment.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [XREAL company profile (this repo)](../XREAL.md) — product specs, prices and dates are carried over from here\n- [XREAL docs — SDK overview (3.1.0)](https://docs.xreal.com/)\n- [XREAL docs — Compatibility (feature support per glasses, host devices)](https://docs.xreal.com/XREALDevices/Compatibility)\n- [XREAL docs — XREAL Glasses](https://docs.xreal.com/XREALDevices/XREAL%20Glasses)\n- [XREAL docs — Access RGB Camera](https://docs.xreal.com/Camera/Access%20RGB%20Camera)\n- [XREAL docs — First Person View](https://docs.xreal.com/Tools/First%20Person%20View)\n- [XREAL docs — Sample Code](https://docs.xreal.com/Sample%20Code)\n- [XREAL docs — Tools](https://docs.xreal.com/category/tools)\n- [Android Developers — Develop with Unity for Android XR](https://developer.android.com/develop/xr/unity)\n- [GitHub — nreal-ai](https://github.com/nreal-ai)\n- [arXiv 2604.08534 — ActiveGlasses](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.08534)\n","XREAL-T02-3-Playbook":"---\ntype: challenge-playbook\ncompany: \"XREAL\"\nslug: XREAL\nchallenge: T02\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t02, playbook]\n---\n# XREAL × T02 — Hackathon Playbook\n\n⬆ [XREAL profile](../XREAL.md) · [Brief](XREAL-T02-1-Brief.md) · [Products and solutions](XREAL-T02-2-Products-and-Solutions.md)\n\n**Ground rule:** the robot does the T02 work (scout, inspect, patrol); XREAL makes the human in the loop faster and hands-free. Build the robot pipeline first, add the glasses second. Robot-side details below are generic — check the other vendor's profile for its real SDK.\n\n| Concept | XREAL device needed | XREAL code needed | Risk |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 Head-up scout console | Any glasses | None | Low |\n| 2 Look-to-steer camera | One + Eye or Air 2 Ultra + Android host | Small Unity app | Medium |\n| 3 Two-way responder link | One + Eye + Beam Pro | Unity app from samples | Medium |\n| 4 Anchored hazard twin | Air 2 Ultra or AURA | Unity app + frame alignment | High |\n\n## Solution approaches\n\n### Concept 1 — Head-up scout console\n\n- **Problem → value:** an operator sending a quadruped into a tunnel, avalanche-debris edge or flooded plant room must watch video, map and alerts while driving. A tablet takes eyes and one hand away. Glasses keep **eyes up and both hands on the controller**.\n- **Hardware:** One Pro / 1 Pro+ / 1S plugged into the operator laptop (USB-C DisplayPort — confirm the port supports DP Alt Mode). Robot: Unitree Go2 / B2, AGIBOT D1, Magiclab MagicDog or Genisom M1 / L1. Optional: D-Robotics RDK on the robot for on-board detection.\n- **Architecture / stack:**\n  - Perception: robot camera (+ thermal / gas sensor if you have one) → detector on robot or laptop (person, smoke, fallen object).\n  - Planning: waypoint patrol or manual drive; alerts raised by the detector.\n  - Control: gamepad through the robot vendor's SDK.\n  - UI: one full-screen web page or RViz layout — video centre, mini-map and alert list at the edges, large fonts. The glasses are just the second monitor. Compute: laptop + robot.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: get robot video on the laptop; confirm glasses work as an external display; sketch the HUD layout.\n  - H6–24: detector + alert logic; HUD page with video, map, alert feed.\n  - H24–40: tune the layout for see-through optics (dark background, high contrast, nothing important in the corners); run the full scenario repeatedly.\n  - H40–48: rehearse; record a backup video; mirror the glasses view to a projector for judges.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) the scenario — \"rockfall has closed the access tunnel\". (0:30) operator puts on glasses, picks up the gamepad, robot enters the mock tunnel. (1:30) robot finds a \"casualty\" dummy / heat source; alert pops in the HUD; judges see the mirrored view. (2:30) operator marks the location and hands over to the rescue team.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** laptop port lacks DP Alt Mode → use a different laptop or an HDMI-to-USB-C adapter (ask XREAL staff). Glare → 1 Pro+ or clip-on shade. If the glasses fail entirely, the same UI runs on the laptop screen.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation low; technical depth sits in the robot / perception side; impact is honest and practical; Leukerbad relevance: tunnel, rockfall and avalanche scouting where a responder must stay mobile.\n\n### Concept 2 — Look-to-steer camera\n\n- **Problem → value:** inspecting a structure (cable-car pylon, bridge underside, pipe gallery) by joystick-panning a camera is slow. Let the inspector **look around naturally**: head pose drives the robot's camera or head, video returns to the glasses.\n- **Hardware:** One Pro + XREAL Eye (6DoF) or Air 2 Ultra, on Beam Pro / Samsung S24–S25. Robot with a steerable view: a humanoid head (Unitree G1 / H1, AGIBOT), a quadruped whose body pitch / yaw you command, or a camera on a small arm (Unitree Z1, Dobot, Flexiv Rizon — the configuration the ActiveGlasses paper used).\n- **Architecture / stack:**\n  - Input: Unity app (XREAL SDK 3.1.0) reads the head pose from the XR Origin camera every frame → UDP / WebSocket (or ROS-TCP-Connector) → ROS 2 node on the robot computer.\n  - Planning: map head yaw / pitch to joint or body targets; rate-limit and clamp; dead-man switch on the phone controller.\n  - Control: robot vendor SDK executes targets.\n  - Feedback: robot camera → low-latency stream (MJPEG or WebRTC) → textured quad in the Unity scene, head-locked. Compute: robot + Android host; no cloud.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: build and deploy the SDK sample to the host; log head pose over Wi-Fi to a laptop. In parallel, command the robot's view from a script.\n  - H6–24: close the loop (pose → robot); get video into the Unity quad; measure end-to-end latency.\n  - H24–40: smoothing, limits, recentre gesture; add an \"inspection snapshot\" button that saves frame + pose; optional defect detector overlay.\n  - H40–48: rehearse; prepare fallback.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) show the joystick way on video — slow. (0:30) inspector puts on glasses; robot camera mirrors their head live. (1:30) inspector finds a planted \"crack\" marker on a mock pylon, taps to capture; report line appears. (2:30) show latency number and the safety clamp.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** video latency causes discomfort → keep the video in a fixed window rather than full-view, and move the camera slowly. No Eye / Air 2 Ultra available → use 3DoF rotation only (enough for pan-tilt). Unity build trouble → fall back to Concept 1 and steer with the gamepad.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation medium; technical depth good (closed loop, latency budget, safety limits); impact — faster remote inspection without rope access; Leukerbad relevance: cable cars, avalanche barriers, thermal-water infrastructure on steep ground.\n\n### Concept 3 — Two-way responder link\n\n- **Problem → value:** in an incident, the person on the ground and the command post see different things. Give the responder a **robot scout's findings in their view**, and give command the **responder's first-person video** — hands-free on both ends.\n- **Hardware:** One Pro + XREAL Eye + Beam Pro on the responder; Windows laptop as command post; a scout robot (quadruped) searching ahead. Optional: SKYLANDX MetaCam scan of the venue as the shared map.\n- **Architecture / stack:**\n  - Uplink: the SDK's First Person View streaming sample sends the responder's view (real-world video + overlays) to the StreamingReceiver app on the command laptop over a dedicated Wi-Fi network.\n  - Scout: robot runs detection (person, heat, obstacle) and publishes findings with map coordinates.\n  - Downlink: small relay server (WebSocket) pushes findings, a 2D mini-map and command-post text / arrows to the Unity app; shown as head-locked HUD cards (One + Eye has no spatial anchors — keep overlays screen-space, with a compass-style bearing arrow from the 6DoF pose).\n  - Optional AI: run a detector on Eye RGB frames (read as YUV textures, forwarded to the laptop) to flag hazards in the responder's own view.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: run the FirstPersonViewStreamingCast sample end-to-end on a hotspot; robot team gets detections + positions.\n  - H6–24: relay server; HUD cards and mini-map in Unity; command-post page with POV video + map + \"send instruction\" box.\n  - H24–40: bearing arrow from head pose; scenario script; network hardening (own router, fixed IPs).\n  - H40–48: rehearse with a split-screen view for judges.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) split screen: command post left, responder POV right. (0:30) robot scout enters \"smoke-filled corridor\", reports a casualty at a map position. (1:15) card + bearing arrow appear in the responder's glasses; responder walks, hands free carrying kit. (2:00) command sees the POV, sends \"valve on your left — close it\"; responder complies. (2:40) recap.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** streaming is LAN-only and sensitive to busy Wi-Fi (docs say so) → bring your own router / hotspot. Protocol is undocumented, so re-using the stream in your own pipeline may not work → show the receiver window side by side instead. If Eye is unavailable → drop the uplink; keep the downlink HUD.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation medium (human–robot–command triangle); technical depth moderate; impact strong and easy to grasp; Leukerbad relevance: volunteer mountain rescue and clinic evacuation, where few responders cover a lot of ground.\n\n### Concept 4 — Anchored hazard twin\n\n- **Problem → value:** a map on a screen forces responders to translate \"3 m behind that wall\". Show robot-detected hazards and the robot's live position as **world-locked markers in the real scene** — an \"X-ray\" of what the scout found.\n- **Hardware:** Air 2 Ultra (anchors, image tracking, hand tracking) on Beam Pro, or AURA if on site. Scout robot with localisation (quadruped with LiDAR). SKYLANDX MetaCam for a venue point cloud. Printed fiducial markers.\n- **Architecture / stack:**\n  - Mapping: MetaCam scan or the robot's SLAM map defines the world frame.\n  - Alignment: fix a printed image marker at a surveyed pose in that map; the glasses detect it (image tracking) → compute map-to-glasses transform → create a spatial anchor so alignment survives when the marker leaves view.\n  - Perception / planning: robot patrols, detects hazards, publishes poses in the map frame.\n  - Display: Unity app subscribes (ROS-TCP-Connector / WebSocket) and renders hazard pins, robot ghost and safe-route line in place. Hand pinch or phone controller to acknowledge a hazard or send the robot to a point.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: confirm device availability; run Spatial Anchor and Image Tracking samples; robot team gets a map and pose stream.\n  - H6–24: marker-based alignment; show a robot \"ghost\" that matches the real robot in line of sight (this is the acceptance test).\n  - H24–40: hazard pins, occluded-robot view, point-to-send command; drift re-alignment on marker re-sighting.\n  - H40–48: rehearse; record a through-the-lens capture (Air 2 Ultra records virtual content only — film a composite or mirror the app view).\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) responder at the door of an \"unsafe building\". (0:30) looks at the marker; the scene aligns. (1:00) robot disappears behind a partition — its ghost stays visible. (1:45) robot flags a \"gas leak\"; a pin appears through the wall; responder pinches to dispatch the robot to a second room. (2:30) show the same pins in the 3D twin on the command screen.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** alignment drift or poor tracking in low-texture / dim spaces → more markers, small demo area. Air 2 Ultra or AURA not on site → concept is not feasible on One + Eye; drop to Concept 3. AURA needs a separate Android XR project (Unity 6) — do not switch stacks mid-hackathon.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation high; technical depth high (multi-frame registration, shared spatial state); impact credible for search and inspection; Leukerbad relevance: locating people or leaks inside buildings and galleries after an avalanche or rockfall.\n\n## Cross-vendor combos worth considering\n\n| Combo | What it enables | Integration effort |\n|---|---|---|\n| XREAL (display only) + any quadruped (Unitree, AGIBOT D1, Magiclab, Genisom) | Hands-free scout console (Concept 1) | **Low** — external monitor |\n| XREAL One + Eye + humanoid head or arm-mounted camera (Unitree, Dobot, Flexiv) | Head-driven remote inspection (Concept 2) | **Medium** — Unity app + pose bridge + video return |\n| XREAL One + Eye + quadruped + Windows command laptop | Responder ↔ command ↔ robot link (Concept 3) | **Medium** — SDK sample + relay server |\n| XREAL Air 2 Ultra / AURA + SKYLANDX MetaCam + quadruped | World-anchored hazard twin (Concept 4) | **High** — frame alignment across three systems |\n| XREAL + D-Robotics RDK | On-robot detection and video encoding feeding the HUD | **Low–Medium** — standard edge-AI pipeline |\n| XREAL + OriginFlow (EMG) | Hands-busy command input (muscle gesture) alongside the HUD | **High** — two novel input stacks; ⚠️ check OriginFlow's real-time API |\n| XREAL + XR-teleop of a mobile manipulator (Galaxea, Galbot, AGIBOT G1) | Remote valve / switch operation in a hazard zone | **High** — community XR-teleop stacks target Quest / Pico, not XREAL |\n\n## Questions to ask the XREAL engineers on site\n\n1. Which glasses, how many XREAL Eye modules, and which host devices (Beam Pro / Samsung) can a team borrow — and for how long?\n2. Are Air 2 Ultra or AURA units here? If AURA: is app camera access enabled, and is there a ready Unity 6 project?\n3. Does 1 Pro+ work with XREAL Eye and SDK 3.1.0 for 6DoF, or only One / One Pro / 1S?\n4. Eye RGB frames: what resolution, frame rate and latency do apps get, and can we get camera intrinsics?\n5. Can a laptop (Windows / Linux / macOS) read head pose from the glasses over USB, or is pose only available inside the Android SDK?\n6. What protocol does First Person View streaming use — can we receive it in our own app (e.g. OpenCV / GStreamer) instead of StreamingReceiver.exe?\n7. Is the XREAL Marker / image-tracking sample usable on One + Eye, or strictly Air 2 Ultra?\n8. Any ruggedness data — operating temperature, moisture, use with a helmet — and a recommended way to power glasses + host for a multi-hour field session?\n\n## Decision rule\n\n- **Pick XREAL for T02 if** your team already has (or is building) a working robot pipeline on another vendor's platform and wants a hands-free operator or responder interface — and you can either live with display-only use or have one person fluent in Unity + Android.\n- **Avoid if** you need XREAL to be the system that senses or acts, you need rugged field hardware claims, or your concept depends on anchors / hand tracking and only One-series glasses are available.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [XREAL company profile (this repo)](../XREAL.md)\n- [XREAL docs — SDK overview (3.1.0)](https://docs.xreal.com/)\n- [XREAL docs — Compatibility](https://docs.xreal.com/XREALDevices/Compatibility)\n- [XREAL docs — Access RGB Camera](https://docs.xreal.com/Camera/Access%20RGB%20Camera)\n- [XREAL docs — First Person View](https://docs.xreal.com/Tools/First%20Person%20View)\n- [XREAL docs — Sample Code](https://docs.xreal.com/Sample%20Code)\n- [Android Developers — Develop with Unity for Android XR](https://developer.android.com/develop/xr/unity)\n- [arXiv 2604.08534 — ActiveGlasses](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.08534)\n","XREAL-T03-1-Brief":"---\ntype: challenge-brief\ncompany: \"XREAL\"\nslug: XREAL\nchallenge: T03\nchallenge_title: \"Sustainable City\"\nfit: 2\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t03, brief]\n---\n# XREAL × T03 Sustainable City — Brief\n\n**Fit: 2/5** — Supporting role only: XREAL is the human-interface layer (HUD, egocentric camera, in-situ data overlay) for someone else's robot; it has no sustainability product, deployment or robot of its own.\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| Best hardware / platform for this track | **One-series glasses (One Pro / 1 Pro+) + XREAL Eye + Beam Pro** — 6DoF, spatial anchors and app access to the Eye RGB camera via XREAL SDK 3.1.0 (Unity). Zero-code option: any model as a USB-C display |\n| Strongest proof point (real deployment or demo + date) | **None in this domain.** Closest: academic \"ActiveGlasses\" (Apr 2026) used Air 2 Ultra head pose to collect egocentric manipulation demos for a robot arm |\n| Fastest credible 48-hour demo | Human-in-the-loop **sorting-cell HUD**: a robot arm sorts waste, low-confidence items pop up in the glasses, the operator confirms or corrects hands-free, corrections become training labels |\n| Biggest risk | Unity + Android-host toolchain eats the first day; hardware on site is a guess; hand tracking on One + Eye ⚠️ unverified |\n| Pairs well with (other vendors on site) | Dobot / Flexiv / ROKAE (sorting arm) · Galaxea / Galbot (mobile manipulator) · D-Robotics (edge inference) · SKYLANDX (3D scan for energy overlays) · Unitree Go2 (sensor patrol) |\n\n## Why\n- **Adds the human to an automation loop** — exception handling, teaching and verification are where sorting / repair robots fail; glasses keep the operator's hands free.\n- **Eye RGB frames are readable by apps** (YUV textures in SDK 3.1.0) plus first-person streaming samples — enough for \"look at item → classifier → overlay\" pipelines.\n- **Spatial anchors + plane / mesh detection** let you pin robot-collected data (temperature, CO2, fill levels) onto real rooms and bins.\n- **Light and cheap:** 82–95 g glasses, USD 99 camera add-on; no-code display path works in minutes.\n\n## Not a fit for\n- Teams wanting one vendor for the whole build — XREAL has no actuators, no ROS 2 package, no autonomy stack.\n- Outdoor snow / wet work — no IP rating published; tethered to a host.\n- Fully autonomous concepts where a human operator adds nothing to the story.\n\n## Sources\n- [XREAL company profile (this repo)](../XREAL.md)\n- [XREAL SDK 3.1.0 release note](https://docs.xreal.com/Release%20Note/XREAL%20SDK%203.1.0)\n- [XREAL docs — Access RGB Camera](https://docs.xreal.com/Camera/Access%20RGB%20Camera)\n- [XREAL docs — Sample Code](https://docs.xreal.com/Sample%20Code)\n- [arXiv 2604.08534 — ActiveGlasses](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.08534) (via profile)\n\n⬆ [XREAL profile](../XREAL.md) · [Products and solutions](XREAL-T03-2-Products-and-Solutions.md) · [Playbook](XREAL-T03-3-Playbook.md) · [Track overview](../../../Challenges/T03-Sustainable-City/T03-0-Track-Overview.md)\n","XREAL-T03-2-Products-and-Solutions":"---\ntype: challenge-products\ncompany: \"XREAL\"\nslug: XREAL\nchallenge: T03\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t03, products]\n---\n# XREAL × T03 — Products and Solutions\n\n⬆ [XREAL profile](../XREAL.md) · [Brief](XREAL-T03-1-Brief.md) · [Playbook](XREAL-T03-3-Playbook.md)\n\n> **Bottom line:** XREAL sells AR glasses, not robots, and has **no sustainability-specific product, deployment or software**. For T03 it is an add-on: operator HUD, wearable RGB camera, and an anchor-based overlay for sensor data. Everything below is ranked by how useful it is in that supporting role.\n>\n> **Research note:** the web-search quota for this pipeline run was exhausted, so track-specific news searches could not be executed. Findings rest on the company profile plus XREAL's and Google's official developer docs fetched today. \"Nothing found\" means \"nothing in those sources\", not a proven absence.\n\n## Relevant products (ranked for this track)\n\n### XREAL Eye\n\n![XREAL Eye](../assets/xreal-eye.jpg)\n\n**Role in T03:** the piece that turns display glasses into a sensing device — a head-worn RGB camera for \"look at the item\" recognition (waste type, part to repair, meter reading) and the enabler for 6DoF anchoring of data overlays.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Function | Clip-in camera for One-series glasses; adds **6DoF** tracking (SDK 3.1.0+, glasses firmware update required) |\n| App access to frames | ✅ documented: RGB camera textures in **YUV_420_888**; photo / video capture; first-person-view streaming sample |\n| Resolution / frame rate | not published in the docs (capture has Low / Middle / High levels) ⚠️ unverified |\n| UVC webcam mode | Docs FAQ mentions a UVC mode in which the device enumerates as a camera-class USB device; which models and how to enable ⚠️ unverified |\n| Compatibility with 1 Pro+ / 1S | ⚠️ unverified (documented for \"One series\") |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping · **USD 99**.\n- **Developer control:** Unity C# via XREAL SDK (`RGBCamera`, `RGBCameraAndCapture`, `FirstPersonViewStreamingCast` samples). Needs an Android host (Beam Pro or Samsung S24 / S25 class).\n\n### XREAL One Pro\n\n![XREAL One Pro](../assets/xreal-one-pro.jpg)\n\n**Role in T03:** the operator's see-through HUD at a sorting cell, repair bench or building walk-through.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| FOV | **57°** |\n| Brightness | 700 nits |\n| Tracking | Native 3DoF (X1 chip, about 3 ms motion-to-photon); **6DoF only with XREAL Eye** |\n| Weight | **87 g** |\n| Connection | USB-C DisplayPort, powered by the host (tethered) |\n| IP rating / operating temperature | not public |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping (superseded by 1 Pro+) · **USD 599**.\n- **Developer control:** (a) plain external display — no code; (b) XREAL SDK 3.1.0 in Unity for head pose, anchors, planes, image tracking.\n\n### XREAL 1 Pro+\n\n![XREAL 1 Pro+](../assets/xreal-1-pro-plus.jpg)\n\n**Role in T03:** same as One Pro, better choice where ambient light is strong (snow glare, glass façades, greenhouse).\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| FOV | **57°** |\n| Brightness | **1,200 nits** peak |\n| Tracking | Native 3DoF (X1 chip) |\n| Weight | not published |\n| SDK 6DoF with Eye | ⚠️ unverified for this model |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ since 15 Oct 2026 · **USD 599**.\n- **Developer control:** USB-C display path certain; SDK feature parity with One Pro to be confirmed on site.\n\n### XREAL Beam Pro\n\n![XREAL Beam Pro](../assets/xreal-beam-pro.jpg)\n\n**Role in T03:** the pocket Android host that runs your Unity app and talks to the robot over Wi-Fi; one of the two officially tested SDK hosts.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Type | Android handheld with dual cameras |\n| SDK status | Officially tested host for SDK 3.1.0 (with MyGlasses 1.11.0+) |\n| On-device AI compute | not characterised publicly — plan to run models on a laptop / edge board |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping · from **USD 199**.\n- **Developer control:** standard Android (ADB, APK sideload); developer mode enabled inside MyGlasses.\n\n### XREAL AURA\n\n![XREAL AURA](../assets/xreal-aura.jpg)\n\n**Role in T03:** the only standalone option — hand tracking, anchors and an on-device assistant without a phone. Attractive for an untethered technician or guest-facing guide, **if units are on site**.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| FOV / display | **70°**, 1920×1200 per eye, up to 120 Hz |\n| Tracking | **6DoF inside-out + hand tracking**; no eye tracking |\n| Compute | Tethered puck: Snapdragon Reality Elite + X1S, 4,455 mAh |\n| Weight | glasses **<95 g** |\n| Android XR Unity features (platform level) | planes, persistent anchors, depth occlusion, object tracking, bounding-box detection, geospatial (VPS); per-device support on AURA ⚠️ unverified |\n| Raw camera frames for apps | ⚠️ unverified (Unity \"AR Camera\" feature is documented for light estimation / passthrough only) |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ launched 28 Oct 2026, first batch ships mid-Nov 2026 in US / CA / JP / KR; **Europe later** · **USD 1,279 / 1,499**.\n- **Developer control:** Android XR stack (Unity 6 + Unity OpenXR: Android XR package + Android XR Extensions, XR Hands, AR Foundation) — **a different toolchain** from XREAL SDK. Also runs ordinary Android apps.\n\n### XREAL Air 2 Ultra\n\n![XREAL Air 2 Ultra](../assets/xreal-air-2-ultra.jpg)\n\n**Role in T03:** legacy developer glasses with native 6DoF + hand tracking — the only XREAL device with published robotics research use (head-pose capture for robot learning).\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Tracking | Native **6DoF** (two SLAM cameras), hand tracking (26 joints via SDK) |\n| Image tracking | Documented: max **5** reference images, **1** tracked at a time |\n| Raw camera frames | A 2026 paper reports they were not exposed to apps |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipped Mar 2024 (USD 699 at launch); no longer in main site navigation.\n- **Developer control:** XREAL SDK 3.x \"6DoF glasses\" reference device.\n\n### XREAL 1S\n\n![XREAL 1S](../assets/xreal-1s.jpg)\n\n**Role in T03:** cheapest way to hand several teammates or jurors a private HUD.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Display | 1920×1200 (16:10); FOV ⚠️ unverified |\n| Tracking | Native 3DoF (X1 chip) |\n| Weight | about **82 g** |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ since Jan 2026 · **USD 449**.\n- **Developer control:** USB-C display; SDK 6DoF with Eye ⚠️ unverified.\n\nNot ranked: **ROG XREAL R1** (gaming display), **XREAL Neo** (battery / video hub — useful only to extend runtime), **xbx a01+** (details not confirmed).\n\n## What XREAL already does in this domain\n\n**Nothing public.** No deployments, pilots, customer cases, application pages or partnerships were found for waste, recycling, energy, building automation, environmental monitoring or circular economy. XREAL's business is consumer entertainment / productivity glasses.\n\nAdjacent evidence that is real:\n\n| Date | What | Relevance |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2026-04 | \"ActiveGlasses\" (arXiv 2604.08534): Air 2 Ultra 6DoF head pose + separate stereo camera to record egocentric manipulation demos and replay active vision on a 6-DoF arm. Academic, not an XREAL product | Template for teaching a robot sorting / disassembly from human demonstrations |\n| 2025-11-25 | XREAL SDK 3.1.0: 6DoF on One series + Eye; RGB camera access and first-person streaming samples | Makes wearable-camera recognition pipelines feasible on current hardware |\n| 2026-06-16 | Unity support for AURA on Android XR | Standard AR Foundation features on the standalone device |\n\n## What they are building next in this domain\n\n- **Announced, domain-specific:** nothing. No robotics SDK, enterprise / industrial programme or sustainability initiative is public.\n- **Announced, general:** 🧪 AURA rollout to Europe \"soon after\" the first wave; retail expansion in 2027. Strategy is two lines — affordable display glasses on in-house chips and a premium Android XR + Gemini line.\n- **Inference (not announced):** an always-available multimodal assistant on AURA is the capability that would matter for sustainability use cases (e.g. \"which bin does this go in?\", guided repair). Whether third-party apps can feed AURA camera frames to their own models is not confirmed.\n\n## Software, models and data that help for this track\n\n| Asset | What it gives you | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| XREAL SDK 3.1.0 (Unity) | 6DoF, planes, image tracking, depth mesh + mesh classification, spatial anchors (persistent / shareable), hand tracking, RGB camera, capture, FPV streaming | Unity 2021.3 / 2022.3 / 6000.0 LTS; XRI 2.5.x or 3.0.x; Android build (IL2CPP, ARM64). **Do not use Android 16 hosts** |\n| SDK samples (via Unity Package Manager) | `HelloMR`, `BasicImageTracking`, `PlaneDetection`, `MeshClassification`, `Anchors`, `RGBCamera`, `RGBCameraAndCapture`, `FirstPersonViewStreamingCast`, `MarkerTracking` (experimental) | Start from `RGBCamera` + `Anchors` for T03 concepts |\n| Image tracking | Pose of a printed marker → cheap way to register glasses to a robot base or a bin | Limits: 5 images in library, 1 tracked at a time, print < 1 m², quality score ≥ 65 |\n| Android XR + Unity (AURA) | Planes, persistent anchors, depth occlusion, object tracking, bounding boxes, geospatial anchors, XR Hands | Hand tracking needs the `HAND_TRACKING` permission |\n| GitHub org `nreal-ai` | 7 repos, mostly legacy NRSDK / MRTK / WebXR forks; `uvc_android` (UVC camera test, updated Jun 2026) | No robotics or ROS code |\n| Unity ROS-TCP-Connector (third party, Apache-2.0) | Send / receive ROS messages from a Unity app | README badges list ROS Melodic and ROS 2 Foxy; Android-build support and maintenance status ⚠️ unverified — a plain WebSocket / UDP bridge is the safer fallback |\n| Models / datasets / sim assets from XREAL | **None** for robotics or sustainability | Bring your own classifier / VLM and run it off-device |\n\n## Gaps and limits for this track\n\n- **No actuation, no autonomy, no ROS 2** — every T03 concept needs another vendor's robot and your own bridge.\n- **Two incompatible toolchains** (XREAL SDK vs Android XR); pick one in the first hour based on what is physically on the table.\n- **Unity-only and Android-host-only** for spatial features; no Python / C++ / Linux SDK. Laptop use = plain display.\n- **Compute:** One-series glasses run no app code; inference must live on the laptop, robot computer or an edge board.\n- **Environment:** no IP rating or operating-temperature range published; tethered cable; optical see-through contrast drops in bright snow light (prefer 1 Pro+ at 1,200 nits). Treat as an indoor device.\n- **Unknowns to settle on site:** hand tracking on One + Eye, Eye frame resolution / latency, UVC mode, Eye support on 1 Pro+ / 1S, AURA camera access, AURA availability.\n- **Sustainability credentials:** no published product-level energy, repairability or recycled-content data found — do not claim any.\n\n## Sources\n- [XREAL company profile (this repo)](../XREAL.md) — product specs, prices and dates are taken from here; its own source list applies\n- [XREAL developer docs — SDK overview (3.1.0)](https://docs.xreal.com/)\n- [XREAL SDK 3.1.0 release note](https://docs.xreal.com/Release%20Note/XREAL%20SDK%203.1.0)\n- [XREAL docs — Getting Started with XREAL SDK](https://docs.xreal.com/Getting%20Started%20with%20XREAL%20SDK)\n- [XREAL docs — Sample Code](https://docs.xreal.com/Sample%20Code)\n- [XREAL docs — Access RGB Camera](https://docs.xreal.com/Camera/Access%20RGB%20Camera)\n- [XREAL docs — Camera](https://docs.xreal.com/Camera/Camera)\n- [XREAL docs — Image Tracking](https://docs.xreal.com/Image%20Tracking/intro)\n- [XREAL docs — Frequently Asked Questions](https://docs.xreal.com/Frequently%20Asked%20Questions)\n- [XREAL developer downloads](https://developer.xreal.com/download)\n- [GitHub — nreal-ai organisation](https://github.com/nreal-ai)\n- [Android Developers — Develop with Unity for Android XR](https://developer.android.com/develop/xr/unity)\n- [GitHub — Unity-Technologies/ROS-TCP-Connector](https://github.com/Unity-Technologies/ROS-TCP-Connector)\n- [arXiv 2604.08534 — ActiveGlasses](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.08534) (via profile)\n- [Road to VR — XREAL AURA launch and pricing](https://www.roadtovr.com/google-ar-glasses-xreal-aura-release-date-price/) (via profile)\n- [Unity blog — Unity support for XREAL AURA](https://unity.com/blog/unity-android-xr-wired-glasses-support) (via profile)\n","XREAL-T03-3-Playbook":"---\ntype: challenge-playbook\ncompany: \"XREAL\"\nslug: XREAL\nchallenge: T03\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t03, playbook]\n---\n# XREAL × T03 — Hackathon Playbook\n\n⬆ [XREAL profile](../XREAL.md) · [Brief](XREAL-T03-1-Brief.md) · [Products and solutions](XREAL-T03-2-Products-and-Solutions.md)\n\n> **Read first:** XREAL is an add-on (fit **2/5**). Every concept below needs another vendor's robot as the core. The glasses must earn their place by making the human part of the loop faster, hands-free or more convincing on stage. Items marked ⚠️ are unverified and must be checked at the booth before you commit.\n\n## Solution approaches\n\n### Concept 1 — Sorting-cell HUD with human-in-the-loop labels (safest)\n\n- **Problem → value:** robot waste sorters fail on unusual or dirty items; mis-sorts contaminate recycling streams. A hands-free operator resolves only the uncertain cases, and each correction becomes a labelled training sample → the cell improves while it runs.\n- **Hardware:** any One-series glasses (One Pro / 1 Pro+ / 1S) as a **USB-C display** on the laptop. Robot: Dobot CR / Nova, Flexiv Rizon or ROKAE xMate arm with a camera over a mock conveyor / bin table. Optional D-Robotics RDK board for on-edge inference.\n- **Architecture / stack:**\n  - Perception: overhead camera → your detector / classifier (off-the-shelf open-vocabulary detector or a VLM call) on laptop or RDK.\n  - Planning: confidence threshold → auto-sort, or queue for the human.\n  - Control: the arm vendor's SDK for pick-and-place.\n  - XREAL part: a full-screen web / Qt dashboard rendered on the laptop, shown in the glasses (no XREAL SDK). Confirm / override by voice, foot pedal or keyboard clicker.\n  - Compute: laptop; glasses only display.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: verify glasses work as a display on your laptop port; get the arm picking one object; choose 4 waste classes.\n  - H6–24: detector + threshold logic; dashboard with item crop, top-2 classes, big confirm / swap prompt; log corrections.\n  - H24–40: retrain or re-prompt with the logged corrections; measure accuracy before / after; tune HUD legibility.\n  - H40–48: rehearse, record backup video, build one slide with the numbers.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) mixed waste on the table, arm sorts the obvious items alone → (1:00) an odd item appears, HUD shows \"PET 48% / Alu 41%\", operator says \"alu\", arm sorts → (2:00) show the correction log and the before / after accuracy → (2:40) pass the glasses to a juror.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** laptop port without DisplayPort Alt Mode → use Beam Pro screen mirroring or a normal monitor. Jurors cannot see the HUD → mirror it on a screen.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation low–medium; technical depth from the learning loop; impact = fewer contaminated streams; Leukerbad relevance = hotels and clinics produce mixed guest waste with seasonal staff who need help.\n\n### Concept 2 — \"Look-and-sort\" wearable camera that directs a robot\n\n- **Problem → value:** staff or guests hold an item and do not know the right stream (Swiss separate collection has many). The glasses recognise what the wearer is looking at, show the stream, and a mobile manipulator takes the item to the correct container — the human never walks to the recycling point.\n- **Hardware:** **One Pro + XREAL Eye + Beam Pro**. Robot: Galaxea R1 Lite / Galbot G1 / UniX Wanda (mobile manipulator), or a fixed arm for a reduced version.\n- **Architecture / stack:**\n  - Perception: Unity app on Beam Pro reads Eye RGB frames (`RGBCamera` sample, YUV_420_888) → JPEG over Wi-Fi to a laptop → VLM / classifier returns stream + confidence.\n  - Planning: laptop maps stream → target bin pose; sends a fetch-and-drop task to the robot.\n  - Control: robot vendor stack for navigation and grasp.\n  - XREAL part: head-locked label + coloured bin icon; optional spatial anchor on each bin so an arrow points to it.\n  - Compute: Beam Pro (UI, frame grab), laptop (models), robot computer (motion).\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: **gate** — build and run `HelloMR` and `RGBCamera` on the provided host; if this is not working by H6, drop to Concept 1.\n  - H6–24: frame upload + classification round trip (target < 2 s); robot handover pose and one drop-off.\n  - H24–40: overlay polish, 5–6 item types, anchors on bins, error states.\n  - H40–48: rehearse with fixed lighting; backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) wearer picks up a coffee capsule, label appears \"Aluminium → bin 3\" → (0:45) robot arrives, takes it, drops it → (1:45) repeat with a tricky item (composite packaging) and show the model's explanation → (2:30) tally of items diverted.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** Eye frame latency / resolution unknown ⚠️ → fall back to the Beam Pro's own camera or a clip-on USB webcam. Unity Android build problems → use the FPV streaming sample or a phone app. Robot grasp unreliable → hand the item into a tray on the robot.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation medium (wearable perception + robot logistics); depth in the cross-device pipeline; impact = higher sorting quality without training people; Leukerbad relevance = multilingual tourists and spa guests.\n\n### Concept 3 — Energy X-ray: robot-collected building data pinned in AR\n\n- **Problem → value:** heat loss, over-heated rooms and stale air are invisible; audits are slow and end up in PDFs nobody reads. A robot patrols and logs temperature / CO2 / humidity (and optionally thermal images); the facility manager walks the building and sees the readings **anchored where they were measured**, with the worst spots highlighted.\n- **Hardware:** **One Pro + Eye + Beam Pro** (6DoF + spatial anchors), or AURA / Air 2 Ultra if available. Robot: Unitree Go2 or a wheeled base carrying a sensor pack on a D-Robotics RDK X5. Optional SKYLANDX MetaCam for a 3D scan of the space.\n- **Architecture / stack:**\n  - Perception: robot SLAM pose + sensor samples → time-stamped (x, y, z, value) points.\n  - Planning: simple waypoint patrol; anomaly scoring (e.g. deviation from set-point, CO2 above threshold).\n  - Control: robot vendor navigation.\n  - Registration: one printed marker at a known robot-map pose; XREAL image tracking gives the glasses-to-map transform (limit: 1 tracked image at a time).\n  - XREAL part: Unity scene with persistent anchors, colour-coded spheres / heat ribbons, a \"top 3 actions\" panel.\n  - Compute: robot / RDK (logging), laptop (aggregation + REST endpoint), Beam Pro (rendering).\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: sensor pack logging to CSV; `Anchors` + `BasicImageTracking` samples running on the glasses.\n  - H6–24: robot patrol of one corridor + two rooms; marker-based registration; first points visible in AR.\n  - H24–40: anomaly scoring, estimated savings per fix (state your assumptions), UI polish.\n  - H40–48: create a visible anomaly for the demo (open window, space heater); rehearse.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) robot finishes a patrol lap → (0:40) wearer looks at the marker, the room fills with data points → (1:20) walk to the red cluster by the window, panel shows the reading and suggested action → (2:10) close the window, robot re-measures, point turns green → (2:40) summary slide.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** registration drift → keep the demo area small and re-scan the marker. 6DoF on the available model not working → show the same data as a head-locked mini-map (3DoF) or on a tablet. No thermal camera → temperature / CO2 points are enough.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation medium–high (robot as mobile sensor, AR as the report); depth in multi-frame registration; impact = actionable energy savings; Leukerbad relevance = heating-intensive alpine hotels, clinics and thermal-bath buildings (inference from the venue, not a cited figure).\n\n### Concept 4 — Teach-by-showing for repair and disassembly (most ambitious)\n\n- **Problem → value:** reuse and repair need dexterous disassembly that is uneconomic to program per product. A technician wearing glasses demonstrates opening a device once; head pose, egocentric video and (if available) hand joints are recorded, and a robot arm replays or learns the task — the pattern used in the ActiveGlasses research with Air 2 Ultra.\n- **Hardware:** Air 2 Ultra or **AURA** (hand tracking), else One Pro + Eye (hand tracking ⚠️ unverified) with a separate camera. Robot: Flexiv Rizon (force control for unscrewing / prying) or ROKAE 7-DoF xMate; optional WUJI Hand; optional OriginFlow EMG capture for grip intent.\n- **Architecture / stack:**\n  - Perception: glasses 6DoF head pose + 26-joint hand pose (XR Hands) streamed via UDP / WebSocket to a laptop; RGB from Eye or an external camera.\n  - Planning: retarget wrist trajectory + grip events to the arm frame; start with direct replay, attempt a small imitation policy only if time allows.\n  - Control: arm vendor Cartesian / impedance interface.\n  - XREAL part: recording UI, live \"ghost\" of the planned robot path over the workpiece, step checklist.\n  - Compute: laptop (retargeting, training), Beam Pro or AURA puck (capture).\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: **gate** — confirm which device gives hand joints and at what rate; stream poses to the laptop.\n  - H6–24: calibration glasses ↔ robot base (marker); replay one demonstrated motion in free space.\n  - H24–40: one real task (e.g. remove a battery cover with two screws pre-loosened); ghost-path overlay.\n  - H40–48: narrow to the most reliable step; rehearse; backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) technician demonstrates the step on device A → (1:00) overlay shows the captured path, operator approves → (1:30) robot repeats it on device B → (2:30) show recovered parts sorted for reuse and the time per unit.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** hand tracking unavailable or noisy → use the phone-as-3DoF-controller or a tracked tool with a marker. Policy learning does not converge → present direct replay honestly. Contact-rich steps fail → pre-loosen fasteners.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation high; technical depth high (calibration, retargeting, force control); impact = scalable circular-economy labour; Leukerbad relevance moderate (local repair of rental ski / e-bike / clinic equipment as a story).\n\n## Cross-vendor combos worth considering\n\n| Combo | What it enables | Integration effort |\n|---|---|---|\n| XREAL (display only) + Dobot / ROKAE arm | Concept 1 sorting HUD | **Low** — no XREAL SDK |\n| XREAL One Pro + Eye + Galaxea / Galbot / UniX mobile manipulator | Concept 2 look-and-sort with robot delivery | Medium — Unity app + HTTP bridge + robot task API |\n| XREAL + Unitree Go2 + D-Robotics RDK X5 | Concept 3 sensor patrol with AR report | Medium — marker registration between robot map and glasses |\n| XREAL + SKYLANDX MetaCam | Pre-scanned 3D model to align overlays or show a mini digital twin | Medium — export / alignment of the scan |\n| XREAL (Air 2 Ultra / AURA) + Flexiv Rizon (+ WUJI Hand) | Concept 4 demonstration capture and replay | **High** — calibration, retargeting, safety |\n| XREAL + OriginFlow | Egocentric video + EMG as a richer demonstration dataset | High — two capture stacks to synchronise |\n| XREAL + Binabik AI / coprod | Glasses as the operator front-end of their agent / modular stack | Unknown — ask both teams |\n\n## Questions to ask the XREAL engineers on site\n\n1. Exactly which glasses, how many, and which hosts (Beam Pro, Samsung S24 / S25) are available to teams — and are any AURA units here?\n2. On One series + XREAL Eye: is **hand tracking** supported, or only 6DoF? Does Eye + 6DoF also work on 1 Pro+ and 1S?\n3. What resolution, frame rate and latency do Eye RGB frames have through the SDK, and can 6DoF tracking and RGB capture run at the same time?\n4. Is the **UVC mode** from the docs FAQ usable to read the Eye camera directly on a Linux laptop or a Jetson / RDK board (no Unity)?\n5. Is there any way to get head pose on a PC / Linux host without an Android device?\n6. On AURA: can a third-party app access camera frames, and can it call Gemini with them, or is that system-only?\n7. Do you have a ready Unity project (anchors + RGB camera + networking) or a ROS 2 / WebSocket bridge example we can start from?\n8. Are the provided hosts on Android 15 or older (docs warn against Android 16), and are the glasses already on the firmware needed for 6DoF?\n\n## Decision rule\n\n- **Pick XREAL for T03 if** your core robot is from another vendor, your story has a human operator, teacher or auditor in the loop, and one teammate can own Unity / Android — or you only need a no-code HUD.\n- **Avoid if** you want a single-vendor build, an autonomous outdoor system, or nobody on the team has shipped a Unity Android build before (then use the glasses purely as a display, or not at all).\n\n## Sources\n- [XREAL company profile (this repo)](../XREAL.md)\n- [XREAL developer docs — SDK overview (3.1.0)](https://docs.xreal.com/)\n- [XREAL SDK 3.1.0 release note](https://docs.xreal.com/Release%20Note/XREAL%20SDK%203.1.0)\n- [XREAL docs — Getting Started with XREAL SDK](https://docs.xreal.com/Getting%20Started%20with%20XREAL%20SDK)\n- [XREAL docs — Sample Code](https://docs.xreal.com/Sample%20Code)\n- [XREAL docs — Access RGB Camera](https://docs.xreal.com/Camera/Access%20RGB%20Camera)\n- [XREAL docs — Image Tracking](https://docs.xreal.com/Image%20Tracking/intro)\n- [XREAL docs — Frequently Asked Questions](https://docs.xreal.com/Frequently%20Asked%20Questions)\n- [GitHub — nreal-ai organisation](https://github.com/nreal-ai)\n- [Android Developers — Develop with Unity for Android XR](https://developer.android.com/develop/xr/unity)\n- [GitHub — Unity-Technologies/ROS-TCP-Connector](https://github.com/Unity-Technologies/ROS-TCP-Connector)\n- [arXiv 2604.08534 — ActiveGlasses](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.08534) (via profile)\n","XREAL-T04-1-Brief":"---\ntype: challenge-brief\ncompany: \"XREAL\"\nslug: XREAL\nchallenge: T04\nchallenge_title: \"Elderly Care & Rehabilitation\"\nfit: 3\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t04, brief]\n---\n# XREAL × T04 Elderly Care & Rehabilitation — Brief\n\n**Fit: 3/5** — Useful human-interface add-on (therapist HUD, patient cueing, captions, tele-care view) for another vendor's robot; not a care robot and no care deployments of its own.\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| Best hardware / platform for this track | **One-series glasses (One Pro / 1S, 82–87 g) + XREAL Eye + Beam Pro** — plain USB-C display for zero-code use; Unity SDK 3.1.0 gives **6DoF head pose + RGB camera** only. Hand tracking needs **Air 2 Ultra** or **AURA** (if on site) |\n| Strongest proof point (real deployment or demo + date) | **None from XREAL in care / rehab.** Closest: third-party live-captioning app XRAI Glass lists XREAL One and Air 2 series as supported glasses (page checked Nov 2026); academic ActiveGlasses robot-learning work on Air 2 Ultra (Apr 2026) |\n| Fastest credible 48-hour demo | Therapist wears glasses as a **hands-free HUD**: live robot state, rep count, force / EMG trace and captions rendered on a laptop, shown in the glasses over USB-C — no SDK needed |\n| Biggest risk | Hardware / SDK mismatch: One + Eye has **no hand, plane, image or anchor tracking**; AURA is not sold in Europe; SDK apps need a Samsung S24 / S25 or Beam Pro host (not Android 16) |\n| Pairs well with (other vendors on site) | Flexiv / ROKAE (force-controlled rehab arm), OriginFlow (EMG biofeedback), Galaxea / AGIBOT / UniX AI (assistive mobile manipulators), Unitree (humanoid / quadruped companion) |\n\n## Why\n- **Light, see-through, glasses-shaped** — the wearer keeps the real room in view, which matters around frail patients (unlike a closed VR headset).\n- **Zero-integration path**: every model is a USB-C external display, so any existing dashboard becomes a heads-up view in minutes.\n- **6DoF head pose** (One + Eye, Air 2 Ultra) is a free motion signal for head / neck / balance exercises and for steering a robot camera.\n- **Camera samples ship with the SDK**: RGB frame access, blended video recording and first-person-view streaming — a base for \"see what the patient sees\" tele-care.\n- Leukerbad angle: rehab clinics and multilingual spa guests suit captions / translation and therapist-assist overlays.\n\n## Not a fit for\n- Teams that need the vendor to supply the robot, manipulation, force control or autonomy — XREAL has none.\n- Hand-tracked or object-anchored AR on One-series hardware; ROS 2-native work (no official ROS package).\n- Anything needing certified medical-device hardware (status not public) or untethered wear (cable to host is a trip hazard).\n\n## Sources\n- [XREAL docs — device and feature compatibility](https://docs.xreal.com/XREALDevices/Compatibility)\n- [XREAL docs — Sample Code (SDK 3.1.0)](https://docs.xreal.com/Sample%20Code)\n- [XREAL docs — Access RGB Camera](https://docs.xreal.com/Camera/Access%20RGB%20Camera)\n- [XRAI Glass — compatible AR glasses](https://xrai.glass/ar-glasses/)\n- [arXiv 2604.08534 — ActiveGlasses](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.08534)\n\n⬆ [XREAL profile](../XREAL.md) · [Products and solutions](XREAL-T04-2-Products-and-Solutions.md) · [Playbook](XREAL-T04-3-Playbook.md) · [Track overview](../../../Challenges/T04-Elderly-Care-and-Rehabilitation/T04-0-Track-Overview.md)\n","XREAL-T04-2-Products-and-Solutions":"---\ntype: challenge-products\ncompany: \"XREAL\"\nslug: XREAL\nchallenge: T04\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t04, products]\n---\n# XREAL × T04 — Products and Solutions\n\n⬆ [XREAL profile](../XREAL.md) · [Brief](XREAL-T04-1-Brief.md) · [Playbook](XREAL-T04-3-Playbook.md)\n\n**Read this first:** XREAL sells AR glasses, not care robots. In T04 the glasses are the **interface for a human** — therapist, caregiver, remote relative or patient — around a robot from another vendor. Typical robot specs (payload, reach, force control, IP rating, autonomy stack) **do not apply**. What decides your build is which tracking features your exact glasses + host combination exposes:\n\n| Capability (official SDK compatibility table) | One series + XREAL Eye | Air 2 Ultra | AURA (Android XR stack) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Head tracking | **6DoF** | **6DoF** | **6DoF** |\n| Hand tracking | **No** | Yes | Yes (XR Hands) |\n| Plane detection | No | Yes | Yes |\n| Image / marker tracking | No | Yes | Marker / object tracking via Android XR extensions |\n| Depth mesh | No | Yes | Environment-depth occlusion |\n| Spatial anchors | No | Yes | Yes (persistent) |\n| App access to RGB camera frames | **Yes** (YUV_420_888) | not public | ⚠️ unverified |\n| First-person-view capture | App content **and** real scene | App content only | ⚠️ unverified |\n| Without Eye (plain One series) | 3DoF, handled on the glasses | — | — |\n\nThe official table names \"XREAL One Series (RGB camera)\"; whether 1S and 1 Pro+ behave identically with Eye is ⚠️ unverified — check on site.\n\n## Relevant products (ranked for this track)\n\n### XREAL One Pro\n![XREAL One Pro](../assets/xreal-one-pro.jpg)\n\n- **Role in T04:** default wearable for a therapist / caregiver HUD or a patient cue display; most likely model on site (best guess, not confirmed).\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Weight | **87 g** |\n| Field of view | **57°** |\n| Brightness | 700 nits |\n| Tracking | Native **3DoF** (X1 chip); **6DoF** only with XREAL Eye |\n| Motion-to-photon | about 3 ms |\n| Connection | Tethered USB-C (DisplayPort); no battery in the glasses |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping · **USD 599**.\n- **Developer control:** as a plain display — nothing to program. Through XREAL SDK 3.1.0 (Unity, Android host): head pose; with Eye also 6DoF pose, RGB frames, video recording, first-person streaming. No hand tracking.\n\n### XREAL Eye\n![XREAL Eye](../assets/xreal-eye.jpg)\n\n- **Role in T04:** turns One-series glasses into a 6DoF tracker with a forward camera — the key piece for head-movement exercise measurement and for \"see what the wearer sees\" tele-care.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Adds | **6DoF** head tracking (SDK 3.1.0+), POV photo / video |\n| Frame format for apps | **YUV_420_888** (Y / U / V textures) |\n| Recording modes | Blend (virtual + real), RGB only, virtual only; Low / Middle / High quality presets |\n| Resolution / frame rate | ⚠️ unverified (not stated in docs) |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping · **USD 99**.\n- **Developer control:** RGB camera textures, capture to file, first-person-view streaming sample. Whether camera and 6DoF run concurrently without limits: ⚠️ unverified.\n\n### XREAL Beam Pro\n![XREAL Beam Pro](../assets/xreal-beam-pro.jpg)\n\n- **Role in T04:** pocket Android host so the wearer is not tied to a laptop; one of only three officially tested SDK hosts (with Samsung S24 and S25).\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Type | Android handheld, phone-like, dual cameras for spatial capture |\n| Runs | MyGlasses launcher + XREAL SDK apps |\n| Developer mode | Tap the glasses icon 10× in MyGlasses with glasses disconnected |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping · from **USD 199**.\n- **Developer control:** normal Android app deployment (adb). Avoid Android 16 on any host — the docs flag open compatibility issues.\n\n### XREAL 1S\n![XREAL 1S](../assets/xreal-1s.jpg)\n\n- **Role in T04:** lightest and cheapest current model — the one to put on an elderly wearer if comfort matters most.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Weight | about **82 g** |\n| Display | 1920×1200 (16:10) |\n| Tracking | Native 3DoF (X1 chip) |\n| Field of view | ⚠️ unverified |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping since Jan 2026 · **USD 449**.\n- **Developer control:** same as One Pro; Eye compatibility for 6DoF ⚠️ unverified for this model.\n\n### XREAL 1 Pro+\n![XREAL 1 Pro+](../assets/xreal-1-pro-plus.jpg)\n\n- **Role in T04:** brightest model — pick it for sunlit therapy rooms, spa terraces or outdoor walking exercises on snow.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Brightness | **1,200 nits** peak |\n| Field of view | **57°** |\n| Tracking | Native 3DoF (X1 chip) |\n| Weight | not public |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping since 15 Oct 2026 · **USD 599**.\n- **Developer control:** same as One Pro.\n\n### XREAL AURA\n![XREAL AURA](../assets/xreal-aura.jpg)\n\n- **Role in T04:** the only current XREAL device that runs apps by itself and tracks hands — the right tool for gesture-driven patient exercises or hand-teleoperated assistance, **if units are on site**.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Field of view | **70°** |\n| Tracking | 6DoF inside-out + **hand tracking**; no eye tracking |\n| Weight | glasses **<95 g**, plus tethered compute puck |\n| Compute | Snapdragon Reality Elite + X1S; 4,455 mAh puck battery |\n| Assistant | Gemini built in |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ launched 28 Oct 2026, first batch mid-Nov 2026 in US / CA / JP / KR; **Europe later** · **USD 1,279 / 1,499**.\n- **Developer control:** Android XR — Unity 6 with OpenXR Android XR package: XR Hands joint data (needs the hand-tracking permission), planes, persistent anchors, ray casts, depth occlusion, object / bounding-box tracking. Standard Android apps also run.\n\n### XREAL Air 2 Ultra\n![XREAL Air 2 Ultra](../assets/xreal-air-2-ultra.jpg)\n\n- **Role in T04:** legacy developer glasses, but the only XREAL SDK 3 device with the **full feature set** (hands, planes, images, mesh, anchors). Best choice for hand-tracked rehab exercises if AURA is absent.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Tracking | Native **6DoF** (two SLAM cameras) + hand tracking |\n| SDK features | Planes, image tracking, depth mesh, spatial anchors |\n| App camera access | Reported as not exposed (ActiveGlasses paper, 2026) |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipped Mar 2024, no longer in main site navigation · USD 699 at launch.\n- **Developer control:** XREAL SDK 3.1.0 via Unity (AR Foundation, XR Interaction Toolkit, XR Hands) on Beam Pro or Samsung S24 / S25.\n\n### XREAL Neo\n![XREAL Neo](../assets/xreal-neo.jpg)\n\n- **Role in T04:** minor — battery + video hub that can keep glasses and host powered during a long demo. USD 119. No developer interface.\n\n**Not relevant for T04:** ROG XREAL R1 (gaming, 240 Hz) and xbx a01+ (budget display glasses, specs ⚠️ unverified) add nothing over the One series here.\n\n## What XREAL already does in this domain\n\n**Nothing of its own was found.** No XREAL deployment, pilot, clinical study, healthcare partnership or application page for elderly care or rehabilitation appears in the company profile research or in the official developer docs. Note: this session's web-search quota was exhausted, so the check relied on direct page fetches — treat \"nothing found\" as \"nothing found in a limited check\".\n\nAdjacent evidence (third-party, not XREAL products):\n\n| Date | What | Relevance |\n|---|---|---|\n| Listed Nov 2026 | **XRAI Glass** (third-party live captioning / translation app) names XREAL One, Air 2, Air 2 Pro and Air 2 Ultra as supported glasses, with head-tracked captions on XREAL One ([XRAI Glass](https://xrai.glass/ar-glasses/)) | Shows the glasses already serve as an assistive display for hearing loss — a common need among elderly guests |\n| Apr 2026 | **ActiveGlasses** research (arXiv 2604.08534): smart glasses with a mounted stereo camera record bare-hand demonstrations; a robot arm replays manipulation with active vision ([arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.08534)) | Path for teaching an assistive robot household tasks from a caregiver's egocentric demos; academic, not an XREAL offering |\n\n## What they are building next in this domain\n\n- **Announced, general:** AURA rollout to Europe \"soon after\" the first wave; 2027 retail expansion; AI (Gemini) as the main driver for the glasses line. See the [profile](../XREAL.md).\n- **Care / rehab specific:** not public. No announced health, accessibility or robotics programme.\n- **Inference (not a company statement):** an always-on camera + Gemini assistant on AURA is a natural base for reminder, object-finding and visual-description assistance for older users; third parties are more likely than XREAL to build it.\n\n## Software, models and data that help for this track\n\n| Asset | What you get | 48-hour use |\n|---|---|---|\n| **USB-C display mode** (no software) | Any laptop / phone screen in the glasses; 3DoF anchoring handled on the glasses | Therapist HUD, captions, robot dashboard — works in minutes |\n| **XREAL SDK 3.1.0** (Unity package) | AR Foundation / XR Interaction Toolkit / XR Hands based; C# only | Head-pose capture, world-anchored exercise targets (6DoF) |\n| SDK sample **RGBCameraAndCapture** | RGB frame access + photo / video recording | Record sessions; feed frames to your own vision model |\n| SDK sample **FirstPersonViewStreamingCast** | Streams the wearer's view | Remote therapist / family \"see what I see\" |\n| SDK samples for Air 2 Ultra | Plane detection, image tracking, meshing, anchors, XREAL Marker | Anchor instructions to a walker, bed or pill box |\n| **MRTK3 integration** (documented) | Ready UI components (buttons, panels) | Faster large-target UI for older users |\n| **Android XR + Unity** (AURA) | Hand joints, planes, persistent anchors, object tracking | Gesture-based exercises, hand teleoperation |\n| GitHub org `nreal-ai` | 7 repos, mostly old forks (MRTK, WebXR polyfills); `uvc_android` updated Jun 2026 | Little direct value; do not plan around it |\n\n- **Robotics bridge:** no official ROS 2 package. Send head / hand pose from Unity to the robot yourself (ROS-TCP-Connector or a small UDP / WebSocket publisher).\n- **Models / datasets / sim assets:** none published by XREAL for care, rehab or robotics.\n\n## Gaps and limits for this track\n\n- **No robot, no force control, no autonomy** — every physical-assistance function must come from another vendor.\n- **One + Eye is head-pose only**: no hands, planes, images, mesh or anchors per the official table. Body or limb tracking needs an external camera or another sensor.\n- **Tethered**: a cable runs to a phone, puck or laptop — a snag / trip hazard for frail users; plan cable routing and keep the patient seated or supervised.\n- **Host lock-in**: SDK apps are only tested on Samsung S24 / S25 and Beam Pro; Android 16 is flagged as problematic. No official Windows / Linux / Python SDK.\n- **Prescription wearers**: many elderly users wear glasses; corrective-insert availability on site ⚠️ unverified.\n- **Not a medical device**: regulatory status for clinical use is not public; present demos as prototypes, not therapy.\n- **AURA availability**: not yet sold in Europe; on-site units unconfirmed.\n- **Evidence gap**: no XREAL care / rehab reference customers to cite to judges.\n\n## Sources\n- [XREAL profile (general research)](../XREAL.md)\n- [XREAL docs — device and feature compatibility](https://docs.xreal.com/XREALDevices/Compatibility)\n- [XREAL docs — Sample Code (SDK 3.1.0)](https://docs.xreal.com/Sample%20Code)\n- [XREAL docs — Access RGB Camera](https://docs.xreal.com/Camera/Access%20RGB%20Camera)\n- [XREAL docs — Hand Tracking](https://docs.xreal.com/Input%20and%20Interactions/Hand%20Tracking)\n- [XREAL docs — Frequently Asked Questions](https://docs.xreal.com/Frequently%20Asked%20Questions)\n- [XREAL docs — home (SDK 3.1.0)](https://docs.xreal.com/)\n- [GitHub — nreal-ai organization](https://github.com/nreal-ai)\n- [Android Developers — Develop with Unity for Android XR](https://developer.android.com/develop/xr/unity)\n- [XRAI Glass — compatible AR glasses](https://xrai.glass/ar-glasses/)\n- [arXiv 2604.08534 — ActiveGlasses](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.08534)\n","XREAL-T04-3-Playbook":"---\ntype: challenge-playbook\ncompany: \"XREAL\"\nslug: XREAL\nchallenge: T04\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t04, playbook]\n---\n# XREAL × T04 — Hackathon Playbook\n\n⬆ [XREAL profile](../XREAL.md) · [Brief](XREAL-T04-1-Brief.md) · [Products and solutions](XREAL-T04-2-Products-and-Solutions.md)\n\n**Ground rules for every concept below**\n- XREAL is the **human interface**; the robot comes from another vendor. Start the robot work first, add glasses second.\n- Check your hardware at H0: **One series + Eye = 6DoF head pose + RGB camera only** (no hands / planes / anchors). Hand tracking needs **Air 2 Ultra** or **AURA**.\n- All concepts are hackathon prototypes, not medical devices. Test on team members, keep \"patients\" seated or spotted, manage the cable.\n\n## Solution approaches\n\n### Concept 1 — Therapist HUD for a robot-assisted exercise (safest)\n- **Problem → value:** a physiotherapist guiding a robot-assisted exercise must watch the patient, not a monitor. A heads-up view of force, range and rep count keeps eyes and hands on the patient.\n- **Hardware:** any One-series glasses (One Pro / 1S / 1 Pro+) plugged into a laptop over USB-C. Robot: a force-controlled arm (Flexiv Rizon or ROKAE xMate) holding a handle the \"patient\" pushes or pulls. Optional: OriginFlow EMG.\n- **Architecture / stack:** arm force / position (vendor SDK) → small Python service computes reps, range of motion, peak force, asymmetry → web dashboard (large fonts, high contrast, dark background because black is transparent in optical see-through) → shown in the glasses as an external display; the glasses' own 3DoF mode pins the screen in space. **No XREAL SDK.** Compute on the laptop.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: confirm glasses work as a display on your laptop port; get the arm streaming force / pose.\n  - H6–24: compliant exercise mode on the arm (resistance level, safe limits); metrics service; first dashboard.\n  - H24–40: HUD layout tuned for see-through; session report export; voice or foot-pedal control so hands stay free.\n  - H40–48: rehearse with a volunteer; record a backup video through the glasses.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) the therapist's problem; (0:30) volunteer does guided arm exercise, therapist wears glasses while the same HUD is mirrored on a big screen for judges; (1:30) raise resistance by voice, HUD flags asymmetry / fatigue; (2:15) auto-generated session report; (2:40) how it scales in a rehab clinic.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** laptop port lacks DisplayPort Alt Mode → use a phone / Beam Pro or another laptop. Judges cannot see inside glasses → always mirror. Arm safety → low force limits, emergency stop in hand.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation — modest; technical depth — lives in the arm's force control; impact — real workflow pain for therapists; Leukerbad — rehabilitation clinics are the town's core business.\n\n### Concept 2 — Captions and prompts for hard-of-hearing guests with a companion robot\n- **Problem → value:** hearing loss makes care instructions and robot speech hard to follow. Glasses show live subtitles (optionally translated — spa guests speak German, French, English, Italian) plus simple prompts such as medication or exercise reminders, while a robot delivers the item or demonstrates the movement.\n- **Hardware:** One-series glasses + Beam Pro or phone / laptop as host. Robot: a wheeled service robot or humanoid on site (UniX AI Wanda, AGIBOT, Unitree G1, Galaxea R1) for delivery or demonstration.\n- **Architecture / stack:** microphone → open speech-to-text model on the laptop → optional translation → caption overlay rendered as a browser page → glasses as display. Robot task queue (reminder → fetch → hand over) publishes status lines into the same overlay. No XREAL SDK required; an existing third-party app (XRAI Glass) already proves captioning on XREAL glasses, but build your own overlay so robot events can be merged in.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: speech-to-text running locally with acceptable delay; caption page readable in the glasses.\n  - H6–24: robot delivery or demonstration behaviour (scripted is fine); event bus between robot and overlay.\n  - H24–40: translation toggle, speaker labels (\"Nurse\", \"Robot\"), reminder schedule, large-text mode.\n  - H40–48: noise test in the hall with a close-talk microphone; rehearse.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) guest cannot follow the nurse; (0:30) glasses on, captions appear live, mirrored for judges; (1:10) switch to another language; (1:40) reminder fires, robot brings the pill box and its spoken message is captioned; (2:30) privacy and deployment notes.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** hall noise ruins transcription → lapel microphone, push-to-talk. Network down → keep the model local. Robot navigation fails → hand-over from a fixed position.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation — merging robot state into an accessibility overlay; technical depth — moderate; impact — high and easy to understand; Leukerbad — multilingual elderly spa guests.\n\n### Concept 3 — Head-pose rehab coach with a robot partner\n- **Problem → value:** home or clinic exercises for neck mobility, balance and sit-to-stand are rarely measured. The glasses' 6DoF head pose measures them without extra sensors and shows targets in the room; a robot acts as demonstrator, spotter or resistance partner.\n- **Hardware:** One Pro (or 1S ⚠️ check Eye support) + **XREAL Eye** + Beam Pro / Samsung S24–S25. Or Air 2 Ultra / AURA for richer tracking. Robot: Flexiv / ROKAE arm as a moving target or support handle, or a humanoid that mirrors the exercise.\n- **Architecture / stack:** Unity app with XREAL SDK 3.1.0 → head pose at each frame → exercise logic in C# (neck rotation range, sway during standing, head-height curve for sit-to-stand counting) → world-placed visual targets and progress ring → pose and scores sent over UDP / WebSocket (or ROS-TCP-Connector) to the robot computer → robot adapts pace, position or resistance. Compute: Android host for AR, laptop for the robot.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: build and deploy the SDK HelloMR sample; confirm 6DoF with Eye; log head pose to file.\n  - H6–24: three exercises with scoring; pose stream to the robot; robot reacts to one signal (e.g. slows down when sway rises).\n  - H24–40: calibration step, large simple UI, session summary; first-person recording for the demo.\n  - H40–48: tracking robustness test in venue lighting; rehearse; backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) unmeasured exercises; (0:30) volunteer follows floating targets for neck rotation, live angle on the mirror screen; (1:15) sit-to-stand next to the robot arm offering a support handle, counter and sway score update; (2:00) robot eases the pace when sway rises; (2:30) therapist report and limits.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** exercise metrics from head pose are **your own design, not clinically validated** — say so. 6DoF drift or loss in a bright, crowded hall → fall back to 3DoF rotation-only exercises. Unity / Android build problems → fall back to Concept 1 display mode with a webcam pose estimator.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation — glasses as both sensor and display; technical depth — AR app + robot loop; impact — measurable home rehab; Leukerbad — thermal-spa rehab and elderly guests.\n\n### Concept 4 — Remote caregiver tele-assist through the glasses (most ambitious)\n- **Problem → value:** relatives and nurses cannot be in every room. A remote caregiver sees the resident's home through a robot and performs simple fetch-and-hand-over tasks, or sees the resident's own view to coach them.\n- **Hardware:** **Air 2 Ultra or AURA** (hand tracking) for the operator; if only One + Eye is available, use head pose for camera control plus a gamepad for the arms. Robot: wheeled dual-arm mobile manipulator (Galaxea R1, AGIBOT G1 / G2, Galbot G1, Spirit AI Moz1). Optional second pair of One + Eye on the resident for first-person streaming.\n- **Architecture / stack:** robot head-camera video → operator glasses (video panel in Unity or plain display mode) · operator head pose → robot head / camera pan-tilt · hand joints (XR Hands) → wrist targets and gripper open / close via the robot vendor's teleop or inverse-kinematics API · speed and force limits enforced on the robot side. Resident view: SDK first-person streaming sample. Compute: Android host or AURA puck for the operator, robot computer for control.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: confirm which glasses and which robot teleop interface exist; video from robot to glasses; head pose out of Unity.\n  - H6–24: head-to-camera control; one arm following a hand or gamepad; gripper control.\n  - H24–40: one full task (pick up a water bottle, hand it over); latency tuning; safety limits; optional resident-view stream.\n  - H40–48: rehearse the task ten times; record the best run.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) the staffing gap; (0:30) operator at one table puts on glasses and looks around through the robot; (1:10) picks up a bottle and hands it to the \"resident\"; (2:10) switch to the resident's first-person view for a coaching moment; (2:40) path to assisted autonomy using recorded demonstrations.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** highest integration load — two vendors, real-time loop. No hand-tracking glasses on site → gamepad arms. Latency or Wi-Fi problems → wired link, lower resolution. Robot teleop API closed → use the vendor's own teleop and keep the glasses for the view and head control only.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation — light glasses instead of a VR headset for care tele-operation; technical depth — high; impact — remote care for alpine villages; Leukerbad — steep, snowbound access makes remote presence valuable.\n\n## Cross-vendor combos worth considering\n\n| Combo | What it enables | Integration effort |\n|---|---|---|\n| XREAL + Flexiv Rizon or ROKAE xMate | Therapist HUD over force-controlled exercise or massage data | **Low** (display mode) |\n| XREAL + OriginFlow | EMG muscle-activation biofeedback in the wearer's view | **Low–medium** (depends on OriginFlow data output — ask) |\n| XREAL + UniX AI Wanda / AGIBOT / Unitree humanoid | Captions, reminders and robot status for hard-of-hearing residents | **Low–medium** |\n| XREAL (One + Eye) + any arm | Head-pose-measured exercises with a robot partner | **Medium** (Unity + pose bridge) |\n| XREAL (Air 2 Ultra / AURA) + Galaxea R1 / AGIBOT G1 / Galbot G1 | Hand-tracked tele-assist for fetch and hand-over | **High** |\n| XREAL + WUJI Hand | Hand-tracking-to-dexterous-hand mirroring for hand-rehab demos | **High** (needs hand-tracking glasses + retargeting) |\n| XREAL + D-Robotics RDK | Edge board runs vision / speech, glasses show the result | **Medium** (board video output to USB-C needs an adapter ⚠️ check) |\n| XREAL + SKYLANDX MetaCam | Pre-scanned room model for placing guidance content | **Medium–high** (alignment between scan and glasses is manual) |\n\n## Questions to ask the XREAL engineers on site\n\n1. Exactly which glasses, how many, and which hosts (Beam Pro, Samsung S24 / S25) are available to teams — any **Air 2 Ultra or AURA**?\n2. Does XREAL Eye give 6DoF on **1S and 1 Pro+**, or only on One / One Pro?\n3. Can the RGB camera and 6DoF tracking run at the same time on One + Eye, and at what resolution and frame rate?\n4. Is there any hand-tracking path on One + Eye (beta firmware or SDK build), or is it strictly Air 2 Ultra / AURA?\n5. How does the first-person-view streaming sample send video (protocol, receiver, typical latency), and can it reach a Linux robot computer?\n6. Is there a way to get head pose on a **laptop** (Windows / Linux) without the Android host — for example over USB from the X1 chip?\n7. Do you have prescription inserts or fit options on site for testers who wear glasses?\n8. Do you know of any healthcare, accessibility or rehab pilots using XREAL glasses that we may cite?\n\n## Decision rule\n\n- **Pick XREAL for T04 if** your core robot build is already on another vendor and a hands-free view for the therapist, caregiver or patient makes the demo clearer — and you can live with display mode or head pose only.\n- **Avoid if** you need the vendor to provide physical assistance, hand or body tracking on One-series hardware, ROS 2-native tooling, or you have no Unity / Android skills and want more than a screen.\n\n## Sources\n- [XREAL profile (general research)](../XREAL.md)\n- [XREAL docs — device and feature compatibility](https://docs.xreal.com/XREALDevices/Compatibility)\n- [XREAL docs — Sample Code (SDK 3.1.0)](https://docs.xreal.com/Sample%20Code)\n- [XREAL docs — Access RGB Camera](https://docs.xreal.com/Camera/Access%20RGB%20Camera)\n- [XREAL docs — Frequently Asked Questions](https://docs.xreal.com/Frequently%20Asked%20Questions)\n- [Android Developers — Develop with Unity for Android XR](https://developer.android.com/develop/xr/unity)\n- [XRAI Glass — compatible AR glasses](https://xrai.glass/ar-glasses/)\n- [arXiv 2604.08534 — ActiveGlasses](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.08534)\n"}});
